      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>.' 14; 41 {. k '</head>
        <p>.' 14; 41 {. k '</p>
        <p>UAI=ETA.L "</p>
        <p>I bear that the Wolverhampton election will 1 There is, I understand, no disposition on the most likely come off next Thursday week. The ' part of the War Office officials to place obstacles in Unionists have from the first been forcing matters, the way of a revision of the pay and provisioning under the strong impression that the Liberals were of the rank and file of the army, and arrangements unready and likely to be divided. Happily neither have now been completed whereby, at a compare- is the case. There's great curiosity as to whether i tively small cost to the country, the pay will be Mr. Chamberlain will speak. Hitherto he has re- handed over without the deductions which have frained from going to Wolverhampton, and Sir been the cause of so much contention in the past. Henry Fowler has not spoken at Birmingham, and The aim of army officers has been that the soldier hence it has been said they had a private under- shall receive the dear shilling a day which was standing. Mr. Austen Chamberlain will support promised him by the recruiting sergeant, and that Mr. Ciibbons, and Sir Henry Fowler will address two he shall at the same time receive, free, a bstter ra- meetings for Mr. Thorne, but the death of Mr. tion than the beef and bread at present prescribed Heorge Dixon creates a vacancy for Birmingham by the regulations. Lord Lansdowne has,l am told, which will draw off some energy and may, it is discovered how it may be done, but the main point thought, create some Liberal Unionist perturba- of his new proposals will be the shilling a day free tion.. of deductions. This will, lam afraid, not be man- aged without the loss of deferred pay, which is at present the sheet-anchor of the temperate, time- expired man (and there are very many temperate steady men in the army),and the new arrangement will therefore have some disadvantage. Instead of receiving fifteen or twenty pounds at the end of ire years' service, the private soldier will, under the new arrangements, get about five pounds only.</p>
        <p>I find that the War Office is being vigorously urged to take steps, in order to secure more effec- tive disciplinary methods for the Volunteer force. At present the system is so defective that insubor- aination is seldom punished. Of course the offender can be dismissed the corps and proceeded against for the lost capitation grant, but this is not obli- gatory ; and what is more, it is so dilatory in its operation that many a commander refuses to resort to it, For instance,proceedings to compel the pay- ment of the grant must be deferred until the ex- piration of the volunteer year, when the proceed- ings having been so long delayed seem to have a vindictive appearance. It is suggested that com- manding officers should be enabled to proceed sum- marily to recover the amount of the grant.</p>
        <p>From time to time there have appeared intima- tions to the effect that the Treasury Old-age Pen- sion Committee had completed their report, and that it was a document of a colourness natbrre,prac- finally leaving the subject where they found it. I have,however, good authority for saying that the draft report is still under consideration, and I am given to understand that the really deba.eable points have yet to be settled. As to the ultimate result it is best not to prophesy. All that can at present be safely said is. that the respective mem- bers of the committee stick closely to their work, and certainly do not spare time or trouble. The re- port may perhaps be looked for shortly after Parlia- ment meets.</p>
        <p>The eleven votes by which the York election was lost hardly represented the narrowness of the vic- tory. lam told that there were twenty-six per- sonar ion agents on the Liberal side employed at the polling place, and that they were all voters unable to poll because of receiving pay for acting as agents. I also hear that twenty-four voters com- ing from a distance to vote for Sir Christopher Furness were in a train due at York half an hour before the poll closed. There was a mishap to the I engine, which caused so much delay that the twenty-four did not arrive at York until half an hour after the poll had closed.</p>
        <p>--- The gravity of the London County Council fight may be gathered from the acceptance of invita- tions to address the Mcderates by Mr. Chamberlain, the Duke of Devonshire, and Mr. Balfour. The Liberal leaders were not intending to take part in the fight, but they cannot now be excused. They must speak out on the future of London with bold- ness and zeal. Invitations are already under consi- deration. Mr. Courtney will probably be asked to take some part in the education of public opinion. He has considerable weight still with the more in- dependent Liberal Unionists, and they would follow him in an effort to prevent the cutting up of London into ten or twelve pretty branches in order to preserve the City of London from being touched by the hand of reform.</p>
        <p>The probability that the refusal to assist India to pay the war costs may cause discontent has been inclined to to many English minds not at all to take a party view of India affairs. Sir Auckland Colvin has given expression to the same view. He has sad,in fact,that judging from what he has i seen n the native Press and his own Dative correspond- ence,existing discontent will be increased. On the principal point Sir Auckland's opinion is note- worthy, and it is commanding some attention. As the frontier leaves India behind it,the possibility no less than the propriety of charging the cost to India "becomes more and more open to question?' It may seem rather curious to say it, but many large manufacturing districts are keenly interested in the payment of this war expenditure, because it means so much less business.</p>
        <p>1 am delighted to bear that Mr.Villiers has left a considerable legacy to his housekeeper. She was a perfect dragoness in his interests. firmly, but kindly,she kept off intruders or inconvenient inter- view s.3ekers. She had studied all'his whims and habits, and could discover bow he was at any time as well as a physician. On of her great cautions to visitors was not to excite Mr. Villiers,or to leave the moment he seemed wandering. I can speak feelingly on the subject,because at different times I have had cession to experience how faithfully she kept watch and ward over her venerable master. No daughter could have managed the thing better, or shown more affectionate solicitude.</p>
        <p>, Birmingham entered to record their votes. Re wae more sorrowful than angry about the lapse.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Dixon was generally regarded as the last of the true Liberal Unionists in Birmingham. ! Up to tie very last moment before the bill of 1896 he had hoped to see Mr. Chamberlain true :,o his ! former educational views, and he is said to have been soothed by the sayings of some of his old col- leagues in educational work who were in the House when he asked them what Mr. Chamberlain would do about denominational schools. it always re- ceived tke same assurance. It came in I he formula, " Wedo not think Mr. Chamberlain vci!l accept any- thing which cannot also be accepted h 15 friends" Mr. Dixon was undeceived,however, I.sna before the time came when he found himselt co•upelled to go into a different lobby from that in whit Sir. Cham- ber! in and the other Liberal Unioui.t MP's for</p>
        <p>The sturdy policemen, some wreaths, and several idlers, anxious to understand what it all meant, were all that occurred to remind as of Gordon Day. " Why was he called Chinese Gordon ?" a youth asked one of the policemen, thinking of current questions and the epithet he had caught from a by-tander just leaving. The policeman was un- able to say; he shook his head. Mournfully the youth looked up to the statute, and down at the wreaths, and then went his way, mixing up Khar- toum and China. It is a lamentable fact that Gor- don's fame is dying out.</p>
