      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>CLYMER—" After air, society is a hollow thing ?" Cooley—" You don't seem able to get into it, though." "DID you and Dennis Anderson come to terms - in your dispute?" asked a business man of his coachman. "Yes, sor ; and very uncompliment'iy terms they wor, sor." MRS. GRIMSILAW—" He was awfully unlucky to break his leg.' Mrs. Malaprop —"I don't see how you can say so. He recovered £l5O damages, and it cost only £2O to have it set." "QUEER critters women is," said Uncle Jehiel. "When one of 'em starts in to git a man all the others tries to head her off, an' then, soon as she</p>
        <p>gits him, they all turn round and help her to man- age him." Fon a neat mixture of metaphors it is not easy to beat the opening sentence of a recent paragraph in a morning contemporary—" Many arc the pit- falls which lie in the path of a man who sits down to make his will." AN old Scottish matron was one day sadly com- plaining of the depravity of modern times. She wound up her diatribe with the assertion that "Paradise was now changed into a pair o' dice, and matrimony into a matter o' money." Mits. ElnowN—" You don't seem to have a very high opinion of your husband's ability ?" Mrs. Malaprop—" No, lie's a very ignorant man. Last night he spoke of persons acting in concert when he should have known that they only sing at such entectainments." A SCOTCH boy was one morning at breakfast, when he bawled out, "oh, mistress, there's a flea running in the milk, and its legs are touching the bottom." The mistress said, " What, Jock, de ye no' get eneuch o' milk ? " The boy replied, "0, yes, mistress, plenty for the porridge I get." AT a dinner party one day, Sir John H--, whose character was considered not to be altogether unexceptionable, said he would give them a toast, and looking hard in tne face of Mrs. M---, who was more celebrated for wit than beauty, gave "Honest men an' bonny lasses? "With all my heart, Sir John,' said Mrs. M--, "for it neither appLes to you nor me." I,:TTLE BES;iIF, had been offended in some way. She went off into a corner of the room and turned her back on the company. "I'm mad," she exclaimed, sulkily. "I wish I didn't have any pupa or mamma." "That's wrong, dear," said lier mother. "We are the best friends you have.' " Well," said Bessie, still sulking, "I don't mind havin' papa. He isn't here much. But I m gettin' awful Tired of the rest of you." Two schoolboys one day, on the banks of the Yarrow, observed a bird's nest built on the arch of a bridge over a deep pool. They were most anxious to get at the eggs, but could think of no way of accomplishing their object. At last it was resolved that one should hold on by the edge of the bridge and the other creep down and hold by his neignl;our's legs. When nearly accomplishing their object the uppermost boy got tired, and called out to his neighbour, " Hold, bold, till I spit on my hands," and with that both of them fell into the water and were nearly drowned. bAVED I—The ship was wrecked. IWO travellers alone of all the passengers and crew had managed to swin ashore to the rock-strewn island. There was no sign of life thereon ; no vegetation; no nothing. "It is a good thing," said the Profes- sional Humorist, "that I brought this axe with me." " Why ?" said the Comic Actor. "We should starve to death if we could not take the axe and get a few chops." "Not at all," said the Comic Actor. "With me and these boulders here we could live. I admit that a ham is not of much value on an island where there is not much fresh water, but roeks' eggs are nourishing." And the Professional Hamorist, like the readers of his jokes, went apart anil wept. INDISPUTABLII—The desire to tell a good story has been known to tempt an ordinarily truthful narrator to enlarge upon the facts. Mrs. B— enjoyed startling her hearers, and had acquired the reputation of " clinching " her point effectively. When she returned not long ago from the hospital, where she had undergone a difficult optical opera- tion, she had many things of interest to relate, and in the course of her description she said—" it was wonderful I They took both my eyeballs out !" " Oh, Mrs. B—! They couldn't have done that !" came in a ehorons of rernonatrance. "But they did," she ave. r ; they took them out and put them on tiw able. I saw them " No CLOSED DOOR— Tie between the Chinese and Europeans is Is-Plight out by the following characteristic. Iv. I na, private houses are surrounded by a wall, and have no windows looking on the street. N,-.ve:!.beless, there is no domestic ptiyaey in China. N.) one thinks of objecting to the entrance ot petit: :t strangers through the ever-open door. To close the door would provoke the inquiry—" NViist, is lining on within, that they are ataid to have everyone see and hear ?" And from that moment, the social doom of that family would be sealed. The Ocoi- dental question to an intruder would be, " What business have you io keep ice out?" T CI-Criese adage covering this matter is, "It pm would not Lave it known this in do it, do not do it."</p>
