        <p>A CARD. Reverend Joseph Holm, Improved Mexican Pre. scription is the only orieinal guaranteed Rem-dy for all those who suffer from nervous weak ness, exhausted vitality, kitinoy or bladder diseases gravel prostate, ppinai weato,ess, brain fa 4 and '&lt;indeed complaints—discovered in Mexico. It has stood the teat for twenty-two years. Thousands cured. Send a self- addre‘sed stamped envelope to TosEra Amass' REMEDY CO. Bloomsbury Mansions, filn,mhbury Square,London, and get particulars of this f 4 tear Specie free. Mention whether for male or female. Genuine sufferers need only apply. Beware of swindling imitators and ignorant , quacks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>AND NEW.</head>
        <p>AND NEW.</p>
        <p>Dr. Talmage was married at the beginning of the week to Mrs. Collier at Alleghiley City. He had been a widower for more than two years. The statement was freely made on the death of Mrs. Talmage in 1895 that she had been one of the most auccessful female specula. tors ever known,and that she had left her husband a for- tune of 200,000 dollars. Apart from that, Dr. Talmage Was a rich man earning £3,000 a year by the publication of his sermons, and possessing tremendous physical powers as a preacher. Dr. Talmage is the Spurgeon of the United States, and when he threatened to resign his Brooklyn church in 1894 because the receipts were fall- ing off, the matter was discussed as one of national im- portance. When he celebrated his silver postorate a few months later Mr. Gladstone sent his best wishes.</p>
        <p>For ingenious misrepresentation of fact the Chinese mandarin is hard to beat. Several years ago a large suna of money was sent out to China from England in relief of an appalling famine.. But Dr. Wenyon, of the Wee- related m a recent speech that when, he aistShan-tung he fcund there a commemorative arrivedl ey n m mission, column, erected by Government authority,on which this English contribution was calmly set down as " tribute money."</p>
        <p>It is singular how many proofs are coming to light of the madness of Archer, the murderer of Mr. William Terries. It happened that the news of the murder reached Melbourne on the day that Mr. Wilson Barrett made his appearance there in " Claudian." Mr. Barrett was greatly shocked at the sad tidings, and said he wag almost certain that the assassin was identical with the man named Archer who followed him (Mr.Barrett)about with a revolver when he was lessee of the Princess's Theatre,London. One day Mr. Barrett called Mr. Archer into the office of the theatre and accused him of intent to murder. Archer admitted the charge, and said Mr. Barrett was his brother and had robbed him. Then he knew the man was mad.</p>
        <p>The General Post office seems to have lost none of its attractiveness to young women in search of employ- ment. At the last examination for women clerkships in connection with that department no less than 4T3 pre- sented themselves to the examiners ' • and yet only thirty vacancies had been announced for competition I This is by no means an unusual number. As a fact,raore than once twenty aspirants for each aavertised vacant position have entered the examination. It is a positive necessity with the Chinese to have all accounts settled before the close of the old year, and tradesmen sell their goods at any price in order to meet their liabilities. A ceremony is held in their josshouset on New Year's eve for the purpose of settling accounts. They all gather around an open furnace, and the debts that cannot be paid are thrown into the furnace and burned, for debts which are not settled on New Year's eve cannot be subsequently recovered. If it is found later that anyone could have paid his debts and did not do so,he is either killed when the fact is known, or dis- graced by having his name written on his own door as a defaulter. A creditor will often pursue his debtor all night long in order to becure his payment in time. They even resort to the fiction of carrying a lighted lantern after daybreak in order to kngthen the nijlt. The one thing which will protect a debtor from an importunate creditor is the presence of a corpse in the house. Know- ing this,a body will be kept in the house for years, in order that they can be neither dunned for debt nor evicted for rent, should they be unable to pay.</p>
        <p>A very interesting statement was made in one of the London police courts on Tuesday afternoon,in respect to the methods employed by prisoners for communicating with each other. The mentit seems, frequently exchange views in chapel, even in the presence 01 the warder, by movements of the lips,and without speaking at all, and this statement is easily accepted by those who know what has been done in the case of some deaf mutes by the system of lip reading. Furthet,it was stated that by means of a sort of Morse telegrapuic code prisoners could rap cut communication from one end of a ward to another. It is said some years ago two gentlemen used to play whist with any other two who could be persuaded to take a hand,aod vilio made the contents of each other's hands known to themselves by a simple system of raps on the table or on the forehead, or by simple movements of the fingers ; but one should think the system of Professor Morse is now too well known for such a game to be tried without risk of discovery. 1</p>
        <p>It is announced that Ran jitsinhji will shortly issue an Australian edition of his book on cricket. It will con- tain the whole of the matter of the English edition, to- gether with some supplementary chapters on Australian cricket and cricketers. As the rudeness of the Adelaide crowd te the celebrated cricketer appears to have been, in part at least,due to !Borne rather injudicious statements made by him to interviewers—as, for example," Austra- lian cricket crowds are wanting in common-sense," and "the public have been prejudiced against us, and everything we do is certain to be wrong "—it is to be hoped that these new chapters will be couched in some- what more guarded language than that used by the author in some of his interviews.</p>
        <p>THE STRANGE CASE-OF MR. CRESS WELL. A few mornings ago, says the Chichester Observer, a member of the reporting staff mounted his bicycle for a run into Fishbourne. His mission was to obtai n " the whole truth, and nothing but the truth "from a person there, and what he elici, ed is worth reprint. ing. The man to be interrogated was Mr. Henry Cress- well.</p>
        <p>"I've experienced wonders, and that's the truth !" was Mr. Cresswell's reply to the first abrupt enquiry. '4 Why I was as near death's door as possibly could be. Even now I look a lot MAO than thirty-two. Last year about this time I was helped home, having brought up a lot of blood, and had to remain in bed for over a week. After that I had three similar turns. Three doctors tried their skill on me and said .1 had an ulcerated stomach-4 gastric ulcer' I always used to feel great pain after a meal, and they ordered me a lot of ' slop.' and milk food. I was often sick.</p>
        <p>"Well, a few montns ago I Wes persuaded to go to Chichester and buy a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I also wrote to the manufacturers for further advice, which was very kindly riven. I found that with the Pills I could thoroughly enjoy a substan- tial meal. The fourth pill I took seemed to go right to the weak spot. I had given myself up to die, as I could scarcely walk about. Now I can do my work and eat a meal without feeling any pain afterwards. I feel quite a new man. I advise every sufferer to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. I would not be without them on any account." Gastric ulcer is an aggravated form of indigestion, or rather it is the result of indigestion neglected. It begins with a feeling of fulness and discomfort after meals, chest ache, and a constant sense that the food, instead of affording nourishment, only weighs down the stomach. There is a bad taste in the mouth; spots seem to move before the eyes : bilious attacks and flatulence are frequent, and the sufferer becomes a confirmed dyspeptic. Then the walls of the stomach begin to inflame, and then to ulcerate, and blood is vomited in alarming quantities : the end of it may be that frightful disease, cancer of the stomach. Some thousands of people, suffering with indigestion in all stages, have written gladly acknowledging their cure by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, which cared Mr. Cresswell. Not one person was ever cured by a substi- tute, however therefore do not be deceived into paying for anything that does not bear the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, on the wrapper. In case of doubt it is better to send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., inclosing price, 2s. 9d. for one box, 13s. 9d. for six. Among the disorders cured by them are also anaemia, rheumatism, consumption ar.d decline, scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and to resters pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thus have cured many cases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia, St. Vitus' dance, and nervous headache. They can be obtained of chem- ists, but it is important to look for the full name, so as not to risk taking a substitute.</p>
        <p>TRE BRITISH CYCLE MANUFACTURING COMPANY.--Thill well-known Liverpool and London firm have favoured us with one of their handsomely got-up catalogues for 1898. To those thinking ot purchasing a cycle during the coming reason, a glance thiough the pages of this catalogue should be ot interest and profit. All th• vari- ous types of Royal Ajax and Silver Queen cycles are illustrated and particularised. There is also an extensive illustrated list of accessories, such as bells, lamps, spanners. Sm., supplied by the firm, and with prices attached. .</p>
        <p>A shocking accident occurred on Wednesday on Her Majesty's ship Bouncer, cff the Note lightship. The crew were at gun-drill whf n a cartridge prematurely exploded in the breech of a six-inch gun. Two of the petty officers met with instant death, and six other petty officers and seamen were injured—two very seri- ously. The annual Liverpool dog, poultry, and pigeon show was commenced on Tuesday at the North Haymarket. The entries numbered 6,542, and are in excess of any received in previous years. Owing to the agitation caused by the high price of bread, the Italian Government will call out one olass of the reserves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>11: DRAB Ninrss. A very serviceable toque for the present season is made with a crown of black cloth, richly embroidered in a scroll design with black spangles. Rosettes of black velvet rest on the hair, and black quills stand up on the left side. A smarter toque, with a folded turban crown of satin, oriental, or mirror velvet with spotted net, has a cluster of soft black quill at the left side, held up with a lappet of black net, which is embroidered thickly with jet beads and sequins.</p>
        <p>Felt hats trimmed with fur, and with bows and a bunch of grass placed at the left side are fashionable. A charming white felt hat, with a drooping brim, is trimmed round the crown with a wreath of lovely Violets, arranged in a loose and natural manner, with green leaves. Under the brim at the back there are clusters of the same violets, which rest on the hair, but the flowers form the role trimming.</p>
        <p>A very charming blouse is made of blue chiffon, and has insertion of gold tinsel, beautifully em- broidered in colours round the bodice drapery. This *I a very elegant model, lined through with blue silk, and beautifully finished. A cosy-look- ing, neat blouse for every-day wear is of a thick ribbed flannel in a pretty shade of electric blue. - It has a triple box-pleat in front, and between these pleats are sewn rows of very narrow blarill velvet ribbon, ,these stripes being taken over the shoulders and arranged at the back so as to simu- late a vandyked yoke. The basque is edged with three or four rows of velvet, and the sleeves are finished with deep vandykes of the same trimming carried up nearly as high as the elbows. The belt is a narrow black velvet one, and a bow of velvet and small white linen stock-collar finishes off the neck satisfactorily. •</p>
        <p>- new model blouse suitable for day or even- -ag wear composed is of accordion brilliant. The Sleeves fit the arm and there is a yoke and a waist belt of rich silk velvet. Black and white sake, indeed, generally make up into dressy blouses, and 9ne of the most stylish methods of treating one is the following : The fronts are box-pleated into a rather deep square yoke formed of narrow per- pendicular bands of the silk alternating with stripes of black lace laid over poppy-coloured satin ribbon, all joints being hidden under rows of very narrow jetted gimp, and the lower edge of the yoke (both back and front) finished by a frill of lace headed with a slightly broader galon. The belt can be either of red ribbon with jet clasp, or of jetted galon mounted on silk webbing. A good many of the most fashionable blouses have short basques, especially those which are made of rather stout flannel or fairly substantial silk.</p>
        <p>very elegant walking toilette is a long mantle black reloung du Nord, or of black silk velvet, completely hides the gown over which it is worn. The skirt is gored to allow ample room for the areas underneath, but there is very little fulness round the hips, where it is joined on to the bodice Part, and the seam is hidden by a very handsome gold bet, Which is studded with turquoises. Over the shoulders is a tippet oF sable, which forms 89uare revers, aud a high collar, which can be worn either standing up or rollad over slightly at the top,. The sleeves fit closely to the arm, from the vrrist to the elbow, and above they form a full puff. Deep cull's of Russian sable trim the wrists, and a muff of the RRine exquisite fur is carried in the hand. With this is worn a felt hat in a pale shade of biscuit with a crown of turquoise miroir Velvet, and a cluster of natural ostrich tips at the left side. Resting on the hair are clusters of violets. This beautiful model could be trimmed With other furs, such as chinchilla, and it is lined throughout with hiEouit satin.</p>
        <p>Your loving J ENNIE.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Laird,the eminent ehipbuilder, who'had been identified with Birkenhead for many years,and who had Illaors than once held the distinguished position of Mayor of the borough, died on Tuesday at his residence in Claughton. in his 64th year.</p>
