      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WOMEN'S CFI AT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CFI AT.</p>
        <p>Now that the closing ot the Old [toad at Ofborne is an acc.omplished fae.t, Her M,j..,:ty could, if it p1f:74,.!.1 her gpeud the whole44loo of the Court' sojoure in the Lie of Wight, witiaut being seen outside her own domain, and so avoid the unweleome staring of tne übiquitous tripper. The dfficisisat Osborbe breathe very audible sighs of relief wben the Sovereign takes her departure, for on these individuals rests the responsibility of keep- ing within bounds the enthusiasm of the crowd of Yan- k,es whn would fairly mob the Queen during her excur- sions in the island. There is no nation so given to spy- ing and prying into the private affairs of Royalty as the democratic Americani,and it is almost hopeless to make them understand that although they can "do" Windsor Castle, Osborne, being a private residence of Her Majesty, cannot be viewed in the Came wholesale man- ner.</p>
        <p>The marriage of the Princess Feadora of Meiningin (eldest Great-grand-daughter of the Queen) and Prince Henry of Reuss,well,according to present arrangements, take place early in September. Princess Feodora was much petted when small by Her Majesty, who saw more of her abroad than in this country. In an early "Four Generations"Photograple she may be seen resting against the Queen, while her grandmother, the Empress Frederick ; and her mother' the Hereditary Princess of Saxe Mainingen, complete the group. • The little King of Spain whose Saint Day has just been celebrated with the usual pomp, will be twelve years old on the 17th of May, and in four years he will be of age, according to the Spanish Constitution. On the King's birthday, gifts are always distributed to all those chil- dren in the Kingdom, bearing the name of Alphonso. Spain is essentially a lazy country. In all ranks the people rise late and turn night into day, but the Queen Regent—a tall,graceful woman,looking younger than her yeare,sets them an excellent example. Up at seven, her first care is given to her children, and by eight o'clock ahe is already at work with her secretary. It is only after lunch, towards two o'clock, that she allows herself an hour of well-earned rest. And some idea of Queen Christian's thoroughness may be gained by the fact that she no longer speaks German—her native tongue—save to those few Teutons who find their way to Madrid. When she first came a bride from Vienna to Spain, she bad mastered but imperfectly the language. Now she talks Spanish correctly, and has,alone and unaided, ac- Eldred a real knowledge of the splendid literature of her adopted country. When speaking of her son she seldom refers to him as." His Majesty" or "the King," usually it; is " my boy "or "my little one." .She makes no out- ward difference between the three children,but it is easy to seethat her heart is specially bound up in Alphonso XIII. speaking of the gayest capital in the world—in which Queen Christina's childhood and girlhood were spent— reminds one of the Viennese Housekeepers' Union, that was founded and is run,entirelv by wemen.lt combines the advantages of tha "stores " in the way of shopping with a school for training cooks, and a bureau where upon everything. All this says one may itquire within much for the patieece, tact, and temper of Viennese women, The worst of British women is Os ,t they do not readily combine. They are too competitive. Each house- wife is ever anxious to excel her neighbour, and al- thou gn alwaye willing to give advice, rarely condes- cends to take it, There will be no diminution of the pleasure of drink- ing tea on the Terrace of the House of Commons this season, but there will be a dtminution of scenic inter- est is the Members' Lobby and the corridors leading to the library and below be the Terrace. The new passage will divert the freu frou from the lobby and the corri- dors.. Women going into the lobby will do so by the central hall, and they will be carried out as they enter. Apropos of the "cup that cheers," etc., the teapot which the Princess ot Wales always uses when she is at Sandringham is exceedingly curious, and of priceless value. It represents an old Dutchman sitting astride on a beer barrel. Rio cap forms the lid,and a gold tap lets cut the beverage. Tee Empress Frederick of Germany too possesses a very curious little tea service, The tray is made of an old Persian halfpenny, beaten out. The teapot was once a German farthing, and the tiny cupe are made from coins of different German principalities. Women, not content with fonthall.cricket.and hockey, are now commencing to play polo ! We can but regi et the fact. Still in this, ,NA in most ether cases, there is a silver lining to the cloud. If an arrangement has to be made for killing off the superfluous members of our sex, it is well the less wise should be selected. Much has been said of late about the manner in which girls are nowadays allowed to walk and hold themselves, but considering it is the way one sits rather than the exercise one takes that determines the erectness of the figure, the present state of affairs is scarcely to be mar- velled at. When our grandmothers were girls and straight-backed chairs instead of cushioned divans were the usual re • eg places, young women held themselves with a Etvo eieuess that was almost stiffness. Then when they glow -,hey still held themselves like Duchesses. According se well-known physician, the proper sitting position to:vireo that the spine shall be kept straight. And it is necessary, he goes on to remark, to sit as far hack in the Chair as possible, so that the lower end of the spine shall be braced against the back of the seat. If this back is straight, the shoulders will also rest against it, and even if the shoulders have no point of support, it will be found that they do not need it, when the base of the spine Is supported properly. The artificel flowers, destined to adorn evening gowns and spring millinery, are beautiful almost beyond des- cription this year. Roses are so exquisite that they would appear to have been just g ithered, and after the Queen of flowers, white lilac promises to be next in favour. Parisians are wearing enormously high aigrettes in the air, formed of these blooms, starting from a mass of leaves which appear to have been touched with the rime of a frosty morning, which is a great improvement on the black feather vogue, so well to the fore of late. More than one woman at the theatre on these winter rOghts, suggests a first class funeral, without being in the least degree conscious of doing eo. Old-fashioned striped shawls,or the broche specimens our grandmothers u.ed to wear,are now in great request for theatre wraps. The centre of the shawl is foued, then it is cut straight up on one ride,to the centre, and a circular piece is cut out large enough to fit the neck. A fur collarette goes round the neck, anda bout the shoul- ders, and behold—a smart, up-to-date cloak. The importance of some systematic effort to familiar- "ze the public with the cistinguishing characteristics of the different varieties of mushrooms has at length been recognised, and a society for the study of this subject has been organised in the United States. The organisers say that there are about 235 edible kinds of mushrooms to he found around Phiiadelphia and that tons of this delicious food are wasted, because people have a horror cf what are koown as toad stook. Mushr aim culture has teen made a profitable industry with us, but it has teen turned to far greater account in Japan where sta. iistics show that in 1895 the export of mushrooms was 1,780,5971b5. Most of them go to China. Before enamelling a bath, it is necessary to scrub it firt with strong soda and water. When perfectly dry, the first coat of enamel may be appliel. Allow it two • days in which to dry, then put on the second coat. Allow twenty-four hours for drying, an f then fill the bath with cold water which ilvaild he left in it for an- other twenty-four hours. Empty arid dry thoroughly and add the third coat of paint. Hero is the recipe for a delicious sweet. Soak over- night half an ounce of gelatine in twice its bulk of water. Lot it remain all night. Neat morning put it in a saucepan and let it dissolve. Now shred very thin the rieds of four oranges and put them in a lined stewpan with a pint of new milk and a quarter of a pound of loaf sugar. Let it simmer 15 minutes. Then Wein the dissolved gelatine. When mixed it must be strained away from the orange pee Land allowed to become cold. Then whisk well and when it begins to solidity add the juice of the four oranges and one lemon gradually. Con- tinue to whisk, till it is pure white froth. Put it in a wet mould, and turn it out next day,</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
