      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>''L</p>
        <p>it was possible to stop the machine whilst 1 that was being done--In reply to his Honour, 1 witness said be had never met with an accl- dent.—Re-examined by Mr. Evans:Witness admitted 1 , that leis mei had been caught by the knives twice and he had to let it go. It was better to let the flour down when the machine was going because it distributed the flour better. In order to reach the damper or shutter to let the flour down it was necessary to stand on a rail I which ran along the machine at the bottom. The tin I he placed under the machine did not catch the drippings from all the bearings. Notwithstanding the precaution I he took he complained to Mr. S'range. He also put a sack down.—By Mr. Bradbury : That was to cover the 1 water you bad spilled 2—Witness No. An the re-assembling of the Court after lunch, the 1 plaintiff was re-called. Mr. Bradbure intimated that he did not intend to further cross-examine the plaintiff. Plaintiff re-examined by Mr. Overend Evans said it 1 was impossible to keep the floor drv,as the machine con- 1 He was never subject to "mazy bouts" tinually leaked. as had been suggested. He was leaning against the 1 trough in the bakehouse, about a yard and a half from 1 the door, when Mr. William Strange came up to him 1 and told him to say that he was using the scraper when the accident happened. lVitness did not communicate with the Baker's Union respecting the accident, but Mr. ' Strange did. Jonathan William Barrowriaydock-street, employed at the Big Horse Shoe Inn, Horsemarket-street, said he I was employed as a general hand for two years at the de- 1 fendants' bakery. He noticed that the machine leaked, ' and let water on the floor. a George Povey, 16 years of age, said he was employed by the defendants. On the 12th October, the day after 1 the ccident. he was told by Air. William Strange to a put the two bags of dough into the cart, and take them into a field and bury them. Afterwards, Mr. John i Strange came to him and told him that if any of the customers asked him how the accident happened he must tell them that the baker caught his hand in the cog wheels. He had noticed that the machine leaked, and I once 0- twice he had slipped down.—Cross-examined by Mr. Bradbury : Be did not know where the cog wheels were. Mr. Strange told him that the customers had said he had been "lacking " and be gave him notice. He hadn't been " larking " in the bakehouse. senior house surgeon at the Warrington as Innflrrin. Langdale, e',gsdaaildthe plaintiff w admitted about mid- night on the 11th October. and his right band was cut oft He was suffering from lesa of blood and pain. He , was aleo suffering frem a rupture, and the strugeliog which took place at the machine would be likely to cause a rupture. The plaintiff made a statement at the Infirmary. Tess-examined by Mr. Bradbury He had not formed any opinion as to how the accident hap- pened. John Lerimore, a seaman rigeer, 64, Granville-road, Seaforth, said he was an inmate at the Infirmary when the plaintiff entered the iestitution. He occupied the bed next but one to witcese. Turner made a statement to him about the accident. er. -et. James Strange came to the Infirmary to visit the plaintiff, and they had a con- versation in witness's hearing. They were talking for about ten minutes but he could not tell all that was laid. tie heard Mr. Strange say that be would get Turner a new hand, and do his best for him: Turner said he was thinking of starting in a yeast business but he wanted £2OO if he could get it. He would like to take young Mr. Strange into partnership. Mr. Strange said he would do all in his pawer for him. Rachel Turner, wife of the plaintiff, said she went to the bakehouse about ten o'clock the morning after the accident and saw Mr. William and Mr. John Strange. They said they were very sorry about the accident, and afterwards asked her to say that her husband got his hand hurt whilst cleaning the machine, or it would ruin their trade. They also assured her that there was no flour in the machine at tbe time. She saw Mr. James Strange about a week or ten days after the accident,and asked him did he mean to keep his promise with refer- ence to her husband and if so woold he put it in legal form. He told her that he would see her in the course of a few days. He did not do al.—Cross-examined by Mr. Bradbury : William and John Strange did not say to her : "He had his band caught whilst cleaning the machine," When Mr. Strange did not come, she did not con.ider it her duty to Ro see him. John Callow, baker, 23, William Henry-street, Liver- pool,said he was foreman with George Lunt and Sons. , Soho Bake-rig, Liverpool. Re bad been in the trade 27 years, and was acquainted with the kneading machine. of Messrs. Vernon's, the one in lase at the defendants' place. They were worked with a cover on by his em- ployers, and they kept the machine covered whilst in action.and there was less dust. It made it safer for the operative. There was no difficulty in working a machine with a cover on. It was possible to knead a batch of bread without looking at it until finished. It was a source of danger to work the machine without a cover, and ought not to be allowed. When the machine was low the operative was more liable to lose</p>
