      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ACCIDENT IN A. WARRINGTON BAKEHOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE ACCIDENT IN A. WARRINGTON BAKEHOUSE.</p>
        <p>CLAIM FOR DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>----- - Before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes at the Warrington County Court last Thursday,the hearing of the action for £312 by Stephen Ravenscroft Tur- ner,a baker, against Messrs. Strange and Sons, St. Jame -'-street,Latchford, as damages for personal in- juries was resumed. Mr. J. Overend Evans, and Mr. Wingate Saul,barristers, instructed by Messrs. Jenkins and Co., represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Bradbury,barrister, instructed by 51r. F. R. Roberts appeared for the defendants. P. C. Barnett stated that on the night of the nth October be was at the corner of St. James'-street,Latch- ford, near the defendant's bakehouse, when a statement was made to him by a man named Taylor. Witness went to the bakehouse, and noticed that Turner's hand was off. He rendered first aid, and afterwards went to the kneading machine, and saw the plaintiff's hand in the dough. He did not see any scraf.er in the drum of the machine, and did not notice any members of the Strange family in the bakehouse. He saw Mrs. Strange and Mr. William Strange on the footpath near the bake- house door. He did not hear Mr,William Strange speak to the plaintiff, but he saw them together on the foot- path.—Cross-examined by Mr. Bradbury: Mr. William Strange wasn't in the bakehouse, when witness arrived. He did not see a scraper at all, and did net know any i thing about it. P.O. Monks said he was spoken to in the street on the I night of the accident, and went to the bakehouse where he saw P.C. Barnett and the plaintiff. He went to the kneading machine and saw the tips of the fingers on the top of the dough: He saw the machine ernptied,and did not notice that any scraper was in it. The dough was put into bags by the assistants, Taylor and Bancrof . Bancroft got a scraper off the edge of the trough, and , said, "Here's the scraper." The floor was rather damp, I but sawdust had been thrown upon it before he got there. Be saw William Strange inside the bakehouse on one occasion, but did not hear him say anything to Turner. Bancroft used the scraper to clean the knives of the machine.—Cross-examined by Mr. Bradbury : Mr. William Strange was not with the plaintiff when he was leaning against the trough. It was after the plaintiff had been taken to the Infirmary that Mr. William Strange came into the bakehouse. The dough had not been die- I turbed when witness first entered the bakehouse. Whilst , witness was there Bancroft got hold of the scraper frum the trough and said "This is the scraper."Ele did net un- derstand what Bancroft meant by saying that. There was some " red stuff" on the scraper, but he could not swear that it was blood. Witness admitted that he said to Mr. Roberts " I noticed a little flesh and some blood on the scraper." Since he gave that evidenca to Mr. Roberts he had not seen the solicitors for the oppesite side. He could not say whether it was flesh on the scraper because he did not handle it.—Re-examined by Mr. Overend Evens : Witness was then yards away when Bancroft showed it to him. That was before Ban- croft cleaned out the machine. , William Wright, 11, Old-road. Latchford, a baker,said ,he formerly worked for the defendant's. He was with them about five years. Two years ago, he was engaged as first hand at the bakehouse in St. Jamee-street,where the accident happened. He remained there until Octo- ber last, and during that time he worked the machine in question. At the time he left in October, the bearings of the machine leaked, causing the water and harm to fall to the floor. The bearings were greased, and the drippings also fell on the Boor. There was no channel or gutter to carry it away. Witness used to put a sack on the floor in front of the machine to prevent him from slipping. The floor was not safe to stand upon unless he did so. He called the attention of Mr. William Strange to the leakage from the machine on two occa- sions. He could not Rive the dates when be did so. Mr. William Strange promised to have the matter "looked to." Witness suggested that a pipe should be placed to carry the water away.—His Honour , said no remedy was suggested in the particulars of claim.—Cross-examined by Mr. Bradbury : No accident ever occurred to him whilst working thm machine. He never suggested to Mr. Strange that a lid was necessary. He could not work it tf a lid were on. Witness put a tin under the machine with the object of catching the drippings from the ma- chine. If that were done on the other side the floor would have been all right. It was the foreman's duty to look after things ot that sort. He could not say that Mr. Strange had been guilty of any negligence. He had never heard of anybody falling into the machine, In ilifting the shoot th,re was much danger, because the operator had to climb on the machine in order to reach the damper. There was no chance of slipping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>EVERY</head>
        <p>EVERY</p>
        <p>WOMAN</p>
        <p>Should send two stamps for our NEM BOOK containing valuable information to Ladles' bow all Irregularities may be entirely Avoided or Removed by Simple Means Recom mended by eminent Physicians as the only Safe, Sure, and genuine Remedy. " Never Falls, Thousands of Testi- monials.—Mr. W. H. BLA.IiCHARDa Claremont House Forest-road, Daleton Loudon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>his equilibrium, and fall into it. The machihes were liable to leak when not properly packed.—Cross- examined by Mr. Bradbury: He had not seen the machine at Mr. Straoge's bakehouse. He did not agree with the idea that tine could catch the drippings. AR machines ought Le be covered in where it was practic- able. He had seen four machines with lids. He would not,dispute the statement that the lids were put on with the object of preventing a waste of floor. If the opera- tion of kneading was carried on by means of adding flour and water wen required it would not be possible to have a lid on. At their bakery the:flour and water were measured before sent down the shoot. Lunt' bakery was much larger than Mr. Strange's. John James Caine, 43, Corney-street, Liverppol, said he was district secretary of the Liverpool Amalgamated Unbn of Operative Bakers, and said he had had 21 years' practical experience. He first heard of the acci- dent by a letter received from Messrs. Strange stating that Stephen Ravenscroft Turner, in their employ, had had the misfortune to lose his right hand in the machine. Turner was entitled to sick pay, and it was the duty of the society to inquire into the circumstances of the case. On the 25th October he visited the defendants' bakehouse and examined the bearings of the machine. He noticed that the floor was slippery, and whilst he was there the machine was raised to empty out the dough, and he saw that there was a stream of water running from end to end of the machine. There was more leakage from the machine than he had ever seen from any other, and he came to the conclusion that it was an old one. Witness had a conversation with Mr. James Strange and told him Turner's account of the accident. Mr. Strange replied " Nothing of the kind ; he was cleaning the machine when his hand was caught." Witness asked him how he knew that, but Mr: Strange merely reiterated his statement.—Creso-examined by Mr. Bradbury : He didn't see a stream of water running, but he saw a pool of water under the machine. That was what he meant, This concluded the evidence for the plaintiff. At this stage of the proceedings the further hearing of the case was adjourned until the 14th of February.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BANK QUAY WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>BANK QUAY WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>The above active and popular organisation held its an- nual tea and entertainment on Monday evening. Over 100 persens partook of the appetising fare provided, the tables being provided over by Mesdames J. B. Nadett,T. Ashley, D. Hill, J. Hassan, J. Monks, jun., J. Dale, A. Tilling, M. Ashton, H. Edwards, T. Taylor, and Miss Cowap. The concert was very well attended. Council- lor H. Roberts took the chair, and there were present on , the platform Mr. Dale. Mr. Hough (president),and Mr.T. Ashley (secretary). T Stfie cßpEaTsAtityTe'airnthperierselanbtio f the usualnurs had been his report said that o duringhe character with the exception of the beginning or March, when a week of opecial revival service's were held, and great benefit was derived therefrom. Their services had been well attended. The open air mission band did active saork in the district during the summer months. The members of the Society now numbered fifty. About 28 open-air meetings had been held. At the commence- ment of the season they had a balance in hand of 03. ill., and with 18 collections and other sources their income for the year amount-el to £42 sa. 100. The ex- penditure amounted to £3B 3s. 441. There was a poor fund in connection with their mission, by which the needy were assiated. The balance in hand last year for that fund was £2 9, lit. and the income brought the total to £5 18 30., but out of that the sub-committee had expended £3 03. 3d. He did not thir k they would be loyal to their yatr an. Councillor Crosfield, if they omitted to mention his kindness and generosity ; be was always willing to assist them. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN expressed the pleasure he had in again meeting his friends at that end of Warrington. He con- gratulated them upon their twenty-seventh year of existence and upon their continued prosperity. Re was delighted to know that the walls of that room were not long and wide enough to hold the children. Some day they would find more convenient premises not very far away. Tea parties, he said, performed many functions. They enabled the congregation to meet each ether. They could not meet on these occasions without missing some who were with them a year ago. He how.d that year would be a red-letter year in the history of the mission. He wished them all al' Prosperous New Year, if it was not too late to do so. (AnnlauSe.) Mr. DALII referred to the good work which was being ene.He said a great many people thought it was unbe- coming to be connected with a Working Men's Mission. He was nleased to know, however, that there were others who did not think so.</p>
        <p>The programme WA4 an excellent one, the following contributing songs i—Mrs. Richardson, Mrs, Stan., Miss Senior, Mr. Bloomfield, and Mr. D. Fanshavr. A couple of excellent reading,: were given by Mr. 11. M. Ashton, W. W. Jacobs' " Change of Treatment "Providing huge amusement.</p>
        <p>To lifionnzas.—Afrs. Wilmslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for their chil- dren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. it is pleasant to taste ; it pro- duces natural quiet sleep by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes as bright as a button," Of all Chemists is. lid. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>WATITIINGTON.</p>
        <p>:'7ATIONALIZITION.</p>
        <p>Mrs.Aldridge,ot the bawl Nationalization Society, delivered an address at the meeting ot the War- rington branch of the Independent Labinir Party, held in the Druids' Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday evening. The chairman was Mr. J. Podmore, jun., and in introducing the lecturer, he referred to the question of the taxation of land values, and con- tended that the unearned increment is entirely ap- propriated by non-resident landowners, who do nothing towards increasing the value of the land, while the workers and tradesmen upon whom the development and improvement and of the town en- tirely depends are called upon to pay the rates and taxes. A lecture was afterwards given by Mrs. Aldridge on "Land Nationalization," in which she sought to show, with terse and incisive vigour of phrase, the evils to which our present system of private ownership of land gives rise. The lecture was illustrated by a series of limelight views.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD GOOD TEMPLARS' ANNUAL Ti. PARTY.</p>
        <p>The 24th annual tea party in connection with the Latchford Good Templars was held in the Temper- ance Hall, on Monday evening. About 100 sat down to tea, their wants being carefully attended to by Mrs.Ellison,Mrs.Few,Mrs. DitchfieldMrs.Pear- son,Mrs.Walker, Miss Hough, Miss F. Fletcher,Miss Hampton, Miss Weeks, Miss M. Fletcher, and Miss Mee.An entertainment followed the tea, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, Baptist Minister,occupying the chair. After a hymn and prayer, Miss Brown, Lymm, opened the programme with a pianoforte solo. Recitations were given by Mr. Henry Bleeze and Mr. Few ; songs by Miss Woodward, Mrs. Bamber, Miss Grounds, and Mr, Wood. A short address was given by the Lodge Deputy (Councillor Ellison), and votes of thanks proposed by Mr. Richardson, and seconded by Mr. Ditcbfield, concluded an en- joyable meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ST. JAMS' s ANNUAL 1.:1A MEETING.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMS' s ANNUAL 1.:1A MEETING.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering at t:1-3-ei--;;;;ual tea and entertainment in connection with Sr. James's Church on Monday evening. About 250 persons sat down to an excellent repast which was provided. in the schools. Trays were sent by the following : —Mesdames Lyon, Parr, R, I. Wynne Jones, Slater, C. S. Burgess, Gibson, J. W hite, Hutchinson, R. Davies, Walter Jackson, T. Jackson, Warburton, J. H. Warburton, William Bolton, Edwin Woods, Millwood, Sampson Kenyon' 'N. N Houghton, IL Greenall, Francomb, Stansfield, Tong, COOK, Greenough, Coxon, F. Booth, Sharp, Feed, G. Venn, Percy Davies, "A, Churchman," the Misses Pier. point (St. Austin's), Dowling. Parry, J. Smith, and Messrs' Milling and Co., Pierpoint and Co., R. W. Row son, A. Chandley, and James Tinsley. An entertainment followed, over which the Rev. F. Slater (vicar) presided. Miss Wood sang " Bother the men" and "Stolen kisses "; Mr. Warburton "The storm fiend "and " The village blacksmith " ; Miss Park "Robin Adair" and "Annie Laurie "; Mr.Mill- wood " Cheap Jack " (words and music by Jr. F. Booth), and "The bashful man." The items were in each instance very well rendered, and received merited applause. Miss Wood was encored for her second song. An enjoyable part of the programme was that entrusted to Mr. A. Grounds, who evoked general langhter with his sketch " The village concert." He made a second appearance with his dolls,giving a ventriloquial entertainment of a very amusing caaracter. During the concert Mr. Frank Booth (the organist of the church) played the ac- companiments. The remainder of the evening was spent in dancing.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SCOTCH:IIEN AND " ROBBIE Bunn.'" Thu members of the Warrington Caledonian As.. socion held their annual meeting on Tuesday night (Burns' anniversary), in the Patten Arms Ilote!, Mr.J.A.M'Llregor,the retiring president,in the chair. There nas a good attendance. After dinner had been served, the business meeting was held, at which the following gentlemen were elected as office bearers for the ensuing year :—President,Mr. J. A. Ewart; vice-president,Mr. T. Dinwiddie ; secre- tary, Mr. C. Lyon ; treasurer, Mr. John Devonport ; commit tee,Messrs. M'Gregor, Sh earer,Fle tch er,Hun- ter, Robson,and Dunn. The remainder of the even- ing was spent in the enjoyment of a genuine Burns' concert.</p>
        <p>LAMPS. —GAELAND'S, Bridge-street, Warr' n gton. GREAT SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of Great Sankey School Board was held on Tuesday afternoon in the school- room. There were present—Mr. T. Halton (chair- man), the Rev. E, S. Jackson, Messrs. J. Wright, W. D. Jeans, jun. (clerk), and G. Clarke (schoolmaster). Mr. Clarke retorted that whooping cough and measles were still the cause of several children being absent. The prizes for attendance and good con- duct were presented on the Thursday before the hreaking up for Christmas holidays. The number on the books ending January 15th was 332, present 395, average 2775. and money received 16s. 81. ; ending January 22nd, on books 329, present 302, average 275.2 and money received 17s. The Clerk reported that the balance in the hands of the Treasurer was £2BO Os. 31. STOVBS.:-:Fleating and .Cy)kIng.—GARLIND, Iron</p>
        <p>monger, Bridge-street. Warrington. CHEAP STATIONERY. ALBUMS, Parses, Hymn Bosks Blbles.—JOHN $. GREEN, Bookseller, &amp;e., 34. Bridge-Bt.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL or A rORMBIL Cnonarint. At the Warrington Cemetery on Sunday thi grave closed over the remains of Miss Martha Oxley, youngest daughter of Mr. James P. Oxley, of Mersey-st., The deceased was 21 years of age, and at one time was connected with St. Paul's choir and was greatly esteemed. As a mark of re- spect many of the members witnessed the last rites, and at the close of the burial service sang" There is a blessed home beyond this land of woe," during the singing of which the greater portion of those assembled were visibly affected. The Rev. H. V. Piget was the officiating clergyman. A large nu- her of friends and relatives were present, the chief mourners being Mr. J. P. Oxley, Mr. and Mrs. W. Hulme (Runcorn), Mr. J. T. Oxley, Miss L. Oxley, Mr. and Mrs W. Oxley, Mr. A. Oxley, Miss A. Oxley and Master Jos. Oxley. Floral tributes had been sent by many sorrowing friends.</p>
        <p>For NAA E nnoncitEs. solid silver name brooches lola an name (Government stamped. Presents or Articles of yourself, you may visit DESERT'S, 10, C HURON-ST HET LIVERPOOL, where you can cet hundreds of articles snob as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Electro-Plate, Cut erv,Purses Pocket Knives, etc at wholesale prices. See advertisement 'lllustrated Chronograph Watch, es. lid., etc.) on pate 6 o nis Paper.— (till</p>
        <p>FEND BES.—C ARLAND'S, Ironmonger, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Trrn SmALL 'Bran.</p>
        <p>The Watch Commit-tee conducted their inspection of the second halt of the hackney carriages in War- rirgton on Tuesday afternoon in front of the Town Hall. Half of the carriages were inspected a week ago. The committee have decided not to license the penny single-horse 'buses which have been run- ning for about, ten years. This decision was arrived at a year ago,and the proprietors were given notice that the committee would not license the 'buses this year. This course has been adopted.</p>
