      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FAMOUSI</head>
        <p>FAMOUSI</p>
        <p>FACTORIES :-88 AND 179, OXFORD ROAD, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>RENOWNED FOR TOW LE'S CHLORODYNE COUGHS, INFLUENZA, TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE BRONCH ITIS, CoNsumpTioN, TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE ASTHMA, TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE SPAsms. &amp;c. 130. TOWLE'S CHLORODYAE per Bottle. A. P. TOWLE MANcuEsTEII.</p>
        <p>" Life sad Tins" Supplement to the Fifty- fif th Annual R9port of the Regis Death." trar General of Births, Deaths and Marriages in England" (part II.) has just en issued. The very title of this publication is so densely official that it is in itself calculated to repel probably nine out of ten ordinary readers. I suggests such a wilderness of complicated figures arranged in tabular form and such a vast expanse of dreary letter press, that many people might naturally jump to the conclusion that it is only one more of those official documents which are printed as a matter of routine or red-tape, but which do not serve any very useful purpose. Yet, as a matter of fact,this official "Supplement" con- tains a vast amount of information that is, when rightly read and considered,of the deepest interest ' and importance. A dozen or two of striking aph orisms bearing upon the life and labours and modes of existence generally of the people of this country might be condensed from its pages . One, for instance, is that not half the children born on English soil live till they reach the middle age of fifty-five. Another is that the most critical year in the life ofa human being is the first,or,at all events,that it is the most criti- cal between birth and the attainment of about eighty years of age—in ether words, life is as pre- carious in the first twelve months as it is after a person has attained the ripe old age of eighty years. The healthiest districts in England are found in the counties of Huntingdon, Rutland, and Westmoreland, while; the unhealthiest are those centres of industry and enterprise, London, Staffordshire, Lancashire, and the West Riding of Yorkshire. Manchester seems to be selected as the supreme centre of unhealthiness. By five years of age as large a proportion of inhabitants has died off in Manchester as at the ages of fifty in what are known as the healthy parts of the country. In the case of boys alone nearly forty thousand out of every one hundred thousand die before they are five years old while the proportion is less than eight- een thousand in the healthy districts. It has been very aptly remarked in view of these vital statis- tics that the old-age pension question is not a pressing matter in the larger towns of the Kingdom, seeing how comparatively small a proportion of labouring men ever reach a real old age in great industrial centres. The boy or girl of four years whose lot is cast in Manchester or any similar city will, on the average, only live till he or she is from forty-one to forty-four,which leaves a very wide margin between the sixty or sixty-five years which have been mentioned as the old-age pension limit. At eighty-five there are only fifty-seven survivors in Manchester as compared with one thousand in the country. It is, of course, well-known that the trade or profession has a marked effect upon the state of health and the length of life, and the "facts and figures" in this Supplement confirm and emphasize those which have appeared in many previous reports issued from the Registrar- General's department. The healthiest man in this country is—on the average, of course—the clergyman. In fact, so prolonged is the life of the clergy that Dr. TATHAM. the compiler of the Supplement,expresses the opinion that "much of the most useful work of our clergy is often done subsequently to their sixty-fifth year." After the clergy, whose exceptional longevity may be partly accounted for by special reasons, come the agricul- turists, those engaged in the first and greatest and most natural of industries. For every one hundred deaths of agriculturists, there are 244 o cottonopera tives, 261 of quarrymen, 292 of iron and steel workers, 335 of glass workers, 373 of file-makers, 407 of cutlers, and 453 of potters.</p>
        <p>lla.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>[PIANOS</head>
        <p>[PIANOS</p>
        <p>[PIANOS</p>
        <p>!PIANOS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>This variation in the death-rates in various indus- tries has always been well known, but the public will hardly be prepared for the announcement that the deadliness of certain trade occupations is in- creasing and not decreasing. A statement of this sort is decidedly discouraging after all the factory legislation and sanitary restrictions of the last few years. That the conditions of the cutlers and file- cutters of Sheffield are now worse rather than better than they used to be is attested by the fact that in the latest report cases of alcoholism and suicide are more than double "the standard." In other occupations "potters' rot" and " lead- poison " appear to be more rife than they used to be, and other principal trades which are showing signs of retrogression in regard to healthiness are those ef the textile-worker, the dyer, the printer and the glassmaker. It will be seen from this that so far as this country is concerned the era of sanitary reform is still far from its end,— that it has, in fact, scarcely begun in connection with largo sections of the population.</p>
        <p>A report issued on Wednesday German Progress. written by the commercial attache to the British Embassy at Berlin will not be pleasant reading for those who appear to believe that it is necessary for the trade of other countries to be depressed in order that this country should be properous. The report shows on the best official authority, that Germany has, during the last twenty or thirty years, made remarkable progress in commerce and industry. Mr. GASTRELL, the commercial attache referred to, says that the continually increasing population of the German empire is finding con- tinuous employment by means of commerce, and is new able to indulge in imported luxuries which formerly were practically unknown to the bulk of the people. The Germai.s are a prolific race and there has been an increase of thirty per cent in the number of people in Germany since 1872, but then, on the other hand, foreign trade has improved to the extent of sixty per cent. If one were to judge by the round figures alone it would seem that Germany had in several instances out-distanced all competitors in the growth of its over-sea traffic. With South America its trade has increased to the extent of 480 per cent, and with Australia 475 per cent. No other country has increased its trade with these parts of the world by the same per centage. But then it may be fairly asked what was the amount of the ori- ginal traffic upon which the per centage of increase has been made? It may, we think, be taken for granted that it was extremely small, so that even 480 per cent. may not represent so formidable a total of traffic as it seems to do. At the same time, there is no disputing the fact that the Ger- mans continue to push ahead with untiring per- sistence. In shipbuilding and in the production of iron and steel they are making extremely rapid strides.</p>
        <p>We are inclined to believe that America and Americans have been much maligned in being accre- dited with the origin of the tallest variety of tall 4tories. The current issue of a British scientific journal contains a little yarn which would open the eyes of Brother Jonathan himself. It is as follows : The great vitality of dragon flies is showa by Pro- fessor McLachland, who having struck at, a large aesclana at rest on a twig, the head was seen to tumble down while the rest of the insect flew away in on "undecided manner for a considerable dis- tance. Upon picking up the head he noticed that the insect had been eating a fly at the time." The mandibles continued working as if nothing had happened, and the masticated portions of the fly passed out at the back of the head." Not having any very near acquaintance with the family of " aeschna " we must accept Ose statement, which is given in all seriousness, as a fact, though in swallowing it we may perhaps not be begrudged a grain of salt.</p>
        <p>On all sides of us the development of electricity le proceeding by leaps and bounds. A few weeks ago we referred to the very rapid progress in Man- chester, and now Chester is able to give an equally satisfactory report. At a meeting of the Chester Tcrwn Council on Wednesday it was stated that While the expenses of the Electric Lighting Com- mittee had been £2,400 the income was just £l5 short of .C.5,000—a good profit surely. It is antici- pated that the income nest year will be raised from .£5,000 to .£B,OOO. The result of all this is that tbe price of the electric illuminant is already being re- duced in Chester. There are,of course, some people who think that while electricity is all right enough for other towns it will not do for Warrington. As it matter of fact, however, objections of that kind are hopelessly antiquated. A progressive town such as Warrington now is cannot afford to do without an electrical supply.</p>
        <p>For some time now the Virarringtm Cricket Club has been considering the advisability of purchasing</p>
        <p>its fine patch of green sward at Arpley. This sub- ject has been forced to the front by the rapid de- velopement of the town and the likelihood of the field beiag selected for a more utilitarian purpose than that of cricket. At the present time, the club is subject to six months notice, and in the event of a " notice to quit" being given, much of the Z 2,000 which has already been spent on the field by the club would be lost. The committee of the Club is therefore to be congratulated by every true lover of sport and open air recreation on the suc- -cessful issue to which it has brought the scheme 50 long cherished by the prominent Pllclrtbers of the club, but which from one cause and another has not been seriously tackled until recently. Through the energetic action of the Purchasing (ommittee and the hen. secretary (Mr. E• Unsworth) backed by the approbation of the Inemhors the ground has been permanently secured. The hon. secretary has been persistent in his efforts 41urinq dm last, twelve months to persuade the own,. -4 of the ground to put a once upon it with the object of securing it ia the interests of the club, and this having been done,a company has now been pra c: ically formed of those interested in cricket to ,compiete the purchase. Everybody will admit that this riereation ground has been a great boon to wally of the inhabitants of the town In 1 neigh - bowl' 1)cl, and as the town expande it will prob - ablY ho a still greater boon in the future.</p>
        <p>T14.-re has been a difficulty with respect to the new Police Buildings forWarrington right fr°rn the beginning. At first there was an unwi linitlt-es to face the large expenditure, and the acherne was hung up from year to year until a Itlreim was received that the Government grant 'Would be stopped unless something was done.A per- move was then made by the 'Watch Commit- tee and a block plan was submitted to the Home The difficulty now seems to be that of a Bitt'' The One recommended by the committee— that l'lhind Bank House—has been rejected by the cetori I after an inspection, and three other sites tare ,wen recommended. One of these is situated at gill, but this has been practically vetoed on , account of the enormoue expense. There are there- fore only two others to be considered—the Old Barracks and a site near Museum-at. The former bas been favourably spoken of, and we may yet see</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>the Police Station taking the place of the Barracks, which, as many will probably know, are already in the possession of the Corporation.</p>
        <p>There are certain matters which keep cropping up in Warrington, and one which makes a more or less frequent appearance is the abattoir question. Few people seem to doubt the necessity for a public abattoir, but still an abattoir scheme seems to make little or no progress. At the hearing of an unsound meat case at the Police Court yesterday, the Medical Officer, Dr. Gornall, declared it to be his opinion that an aba- ttoir was more needed in Warrington than new police buildings. This very case seems to prove that a public slaughter-house is wanted both in the interests of the public and of the butchers. The condemned carcase was that of an apparently soand animal bought at a public sale, yet after death the meat was found to be extensively infected with tubercular disease. The butcher, who did not slaughter the animal himself, and did not therefore know of its condition, has had to suffer all the dis- advantages of a public prosecution, while appar- ently but for the accidental visit to the slaughter- house of an inspector,the diseased meat might,quite unwittingly on the part of those concerned, have been retailed to the public. In cases of this kind an abattoir would be a safeguard both for the butcher and the public. The public would be safe- guarded by the fact that all meat would have to pass under the eye of a trained inspector, while on the other hand there would be no necessity for prosecuting a butcher who had purchased a diseased animal in the belief that it was a healthy one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MR. JOSIAH NIX AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MR. JOSIAH NIX AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE PLEDGE MEETING. CLOSE OF THE MISSION. Bold-street Wesleyan Church was again com- pletely filled on Saturday evening, when Mr. Josiah Nix conducted what he termed a great pledge sign- ing campaign meeting: Several well-known hymns of Sankey's were heartily sung, the singing being led by a large choir under the conductorship of Mr. Walter Brough, which was accommodated on an i improvised platform, n front of the organ. The miseioner was also accompanied on the platform by the Rev. William Potts (superintendent of the cir- cuit), the Rev. G. Harbottle, and the Rev. W. G. Triggs. In the course of a stirring temperance address, Mr. Nix aaid he supposed that every other evangelist in the country took a day's rest on Saturdays, but in his work with the West London Mission with which he was connected for nearly nine years, the question of intemperance was so deeply impressed upon him and played such an important part in his life and work that such a thing as resting on a Saturday evening was impossible for him, He had carefully investigated the cases of backsliding by people belonging to all parts of the country and also from other countries. It was impossible for him to investigate every case, but in some cases he did most patiently and quietly endeavour to Erase the cause of their backsliding, and as a result of his investigation became to this conclusion I —driven to it by stern, solid facts—that there was not I one backslider but who had become such through the intoxicating cup. The experience of others might be different to his, but that did not alter the fact which he had mentioned. Through loss, bereavement, misfortune, or habit, these people had taken to drink, and thrown up their religion. He had seen people, some of whom had been in the highest positions in this country and in the Christian Church, dragged down to beggary and worse than that through drink. In four months he investigated the cases of 1,198 people who were in trouble, some of whom come from the county of Lanca- shire. He had visited the mansions of the wealthy, and taken back the prodigal girl and boy from the gutters of London. He became so depressed with the evil of drink that he made up his mind when he became an evangelist,that no matter where he went or whether the people believed in temperance or not, they would never have his services unless he was at liberty to hold a tem- perance meeting on Saturday night. Knowing the history of the town of Warrington,and how it was in the hands of the brewing interests,and how that influence effected the:election of their public representatives,he suggested to the Superintendent (Mr.Potts)that he had better select another evangelist. Hs felt that it would be better if they had someone who did not feel so keenly or strongly on the liquor traffic,because there was no one who could intimidate him or bridle his tongue on this question. (Appian, e.) The speaker proceeded to explain thet at onetime he was himself a moderate drinker, and had no sympathy with teetotallers.ile was led to sign the pledge at Oxford durieg a mission by the Rev. Hugh Price Hughes, and since he had been engaged in the work of the mission at London he had no hesitation in saying that drink was the greatest curse of this country. He would challenge any man or woman to get up and tell him of one good thing intoxicating drink had ever done. It corrupted society, and business, and produced ignor- ance, pauperism, crime, vice, and everything that was cruel, vicious, and impure. We spent £5 every second in intoxicating drink, or £3OO per minute from the first tick of Use clock in January to the last minute in Decem- ber If they put that into sovereigns they would require 80,000 men,each carrying a cwt., to carry the sovereigns which were yearly wasted on drink. He did not object to the spending of the money but he wortild prefer that it should be spent in the education and clothing of the people than that it should be wasted in drink. Some striking figures as to what could be done with the money so spent were given by the speaker, who suggested that the temperance p3opla in the town ought to provide a beautiful ball where the people could get good recreation and amusement without going to the public-bouse. About 97 per cent, of the people in prison were there directly through the intoxicating cup ; over 90 per cent. of those in pauper houses were there from the same cause. It also accounted for over 80 per centsof those in lunatic asylums, and 90 per cent, of those in the slums. In London alone there were nearly 80,000 fallen women, 15,000 of them under la years of age, and the majority of them had been dragged down by drink. He hoped the time had come when every man with a heart and who was proud of the name of Englishman would feel that it was his bounden duty to sign the temperance pledge for the sake of others. We were not in fear of Germany. France, or Russia, or even the combining of nations together, but they ought to be in fear of our people being destroyed by the intoxicating cup. (Ap- plause.) Drink spelled starvation, destitution, and damnation, and people had no right to invest their money in that which brought evil and ignorance, pan- 'petism and sin. There might be some ready to rejoin that he (the speaker) must not forget that there were a great many people in the town who earne I a living out of the trade, He had not forgotten that fact, but they must remember that of all the money spent on drink only 24 per cent of it went into the working- man's pocket ; that was 451. out of every pound spent. If the same amount of money were spent in the purchase of clothing and manufactured articles as well as food, £58,400,000 would find its way into this pockets of the wage-earning classes, so that they would see that the workingman was suffering through the liquor traffic to the extent of nearly fifty-three millions per annum. (Applause.) Some might ask how we would be able to maintain our navy and ai my and national expenditure without the ditties raised by drink. To that he would reply that they would be in a better position than now. (Laughter.) If they were without intoxicat- ing liquor they could dispense with nearly all the judges, especially in the divorce court. Then there would not be the same expenditure on prisons, workhouses, and asylums, which cost an enormous amount to maintain. We did not want a navy built on beer barrels, or our army fed en grains. The people of this sountry would see to it that these were not neglected even if there were no beer and spirit duties. The speaker gave several illustrations which had come under his own observation of the evil of drink and its effect on promising young fellows. In speaking of the miracle of Christ in turning the water into wine at the marriage feast, Mr. Nix stated that he was once asked by is member of the Li- censed Victuallers Association in London as to who was the worse, the man who made wine or the man who sold it. He (Mr. Nix) replied, the man who made it, where- upon his questioner referred to Christ's conduct at the marriage feast. He (Mr. Nix) then asked what Christ put into the water, and on being told nothing, replied that he would have no objection to the trade of the li- censed victuallers when they made their wine of nothing else but water. (Laughter.) In conclusion he urged all to make a sacrifice and sign the pledge so that by their example they might influence others and save them- selveri from the damning effects of drink.—About 30G pledges were afterwards taken. Wesleyan Church presented a unique appear- ance on Sunday evening. Mr. Nix was conducting the last of his Sundey services anl the spacious building was crowded to its utmost. Round each door people stood in knots,while the aisles were also utilised by the congregation. Mr. Nix preached from the Bth chapter of Homans,23rd verse, " The wages of sin is death and the gift of God is eternal life." From this striking text he preached a powerful and moving sermon, forcing many home truths upon the congregation. After plainly showing that " the wages of sin is death," Mr. Nix turned to the brighter side of his text and said that his last message to the people at Warrington was The free gift of Christ is eternal life." They bad nothing to pay, nothing to do but just stretch out their hands and take it. He prayed that every heart in the Church might be influenced to accept the gift. After the sermon Mr. bitx continued the service till after 9 o'clock, hymn, prayer, and exhortation entering largely mto the proceedings.</p>
        <p>RUN:JoItNi) Wk .)NE.</p>
        <p>HELENS OF OA N BENEFIT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>' Members of the above Society residing in Runcorn and Widnes are earnestly requested to attend a meet- to be hold on Wednesday evening next, in the Ashridge- street Gospel Mission Hall. Particulars will be found in our advertising Columns, SEAMAN'S MISSION, RUNCORN. There was a very large attendance at the weekly concert of the Seamen's Mission, on Tuesday night. Mr. J.K.Digges occupied the chair,and songs,&amp;n. ,were contri- buted by Messrs. T. Woods, A. Shingler, E. Abrams, Captain Nicholls, the Misses A. Bancroft and Rees. A collection was made in aid of the Sailors' Grave Fund. On the motion of the chairman, seconded by Mr. Rose, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the audience for their presence and support.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON ROME.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a lecture was delivered by the Rev. Dr. O'Toole, of Oswestry, in the Catholic Schools, Mill Brow,Runcorn,on the subject "Rome." The lecture was illustrated by limelight views, and on the screen were exhibited pictures of the Catacombs, the Vatican, St. Pater's, the Sistine Chapel, and 350 other Catholic churches in Rome. The last slide was his Holiness Pope Leo XIII., giving the Papal benediction. Mr. T. Bowyer manipulated the lantern. " THE INFLUENCE OF ATHLETICS ON LIFE." On Wednesday evening, Mr. F. Littler read a oaper on the above subject, at the weekly meeting of the Bruns- wick Improvement Society. Mr. T. Simister presided over the meeting: The essayist whilst strongly in favour of athletics, deprecated gambling and drink which seemed to be part and parcel of the present day sports. discussion followed, Messrs. J. Jones, Millington, and W. Leathwood, junior taking part. BRUNSWICK SCHOOL—MRS. SOULBY'S SOCIETY CLASS. In connection with Mrs. Soulby's Society Class, the anneal tea party was held on Tuesday evening in Bruns- wick school, a splendid tea was provided, the tables be- ing presided over by Mesdames Soulby and Simkin. After tea speeches were delivered by the Chairwoman, Mrs. Soulby and Mesdame3 Robinson, Littler, and T. Simkin. Songs were rendered by Mesdames Burrows, F. Ellison, Worrall, and the Misses Norman and Cun- cliffe. Readings were given by Miss Taylor and Miss Millington and a duett was rendered by Miss Dobson and Mrs. Savage. A very enjayable evening was spent, the party breaking up at a reasonable hour.</p>
        <p>- - - SATED FROM DROWNING BY A POLICEMAN. Before Mr. B. Taylor at the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday morning, Niel Loke, a foreigner, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in High-street, Run- corn the previous night.—P.C. Morris stated that at ' 10.30 p.m. the previous day he was at Top Locks when he saw the prisoner very drunk. He was going down the docks and witness followed him. Prisoner fell into the basin of the third lock and .witness pulled him out. He could not tell him where his vessel was and witness was obliged to lock him up. He commenced to shout and main use of very bad language. Prisoner who ap- wared in court with a blanket around him was fined 53. including costs or seven days imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
        <p>ST. EDWARDS LE&amp;GOU OF TIER CROSS, RUNCORN. On Sunday at the weekly meeting of the abovexociety held in the Lague rooms Irwell.lane, the president, Mr. William Woods,gave a very interesting reading on "The Flower Seller" selected trom a sketca by Mrs. Henry Wood. The reading which extended over half-an-hour caused roars of laugoter. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Woods on the motion of Mr. J. Whelan, seconded by the chaplain, the Rev. Fattier Gore.</p>
        <p>BILLIARD Marca—Cazwit V. RUNCORN LinisnaL Cum. On Wednesday a team composed of eleven members or the Runcorn Liberal Club journeyed to Crewe to play billiard match with the Liberals of ,hat town. The fol- lowiug are the individual scores :</p>
        <p>tREWE. RUNCORN. W R Briggs l5O H C Barnes 125 J Lightfoot J Forster R Roberts ll9 J 11 lilot l5O IV Finch F Carr T Williams T Ward l5O B Johnson T Allen S Lowe l5O T Carruthers H Jones 1299</p>
        <p>1487</p>
        <p>A FORMER RUNCORNIAN DROWNED AT Roar FERRY.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Coroner's Court, on Tnuraday last week before Mr. E. Gibson, deputy coroner, an inqaest was held on the body of Eliza Lewis, 53, wife of a water- man, who lived ot Rock Ferry. It was the custom of the deceased, when her husband was expected home, to meet him at Rock Ferry Slip. Oa the 10th inst. the husband went with his fiat to Saltport, and then to Manchester. On Saturday he wrote telling his wife that he expected to be back on Tuesday morning. On that day deceased disappeared, and subsequently her body was picked up in mid-river, opposite the Hercu- laneum Dock, by the river police boat. It is presumed that the poor woman went to the slip to meet her hus band, slipped on the stones, and fell into the water The jury returned at verdict of" Accidental death'- The deceased formerly resided in Runcorn, and the body was interred at the Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. SAD DBATII OP A RUNCORN WOMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Woreley, coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday, relative to the death of Mary Water worth, aged 73 years,a midwife, of 17, Elope-st., who suddenly died whilst attending a con- finement case, at the house of Mrs. White, jun., 28, Princess-st.—Elizabeth Brown, wife of Peter Brown, liv- ing in Hope-st.,said deceased was called out to 28 Fria- cess-st.,en Sunday afternoon, to a confinement case. She was then in her usual health.—iann Thornhill, wife of George Thornhill, waterman, 20 Princess-st., Runcorn, Said she was iat the house 26 Princess-st., on Sunday afternoon. Mrs. Waterworth was there attending Mrs. White, Jun. At 0 30 p.m the deceased was attending the case when she fell forward on the bed. Witness thought she had fainted away, and she went to her assistance. Deceased had had nothing to eat in White's house. She had a cup of tea. She remarked that she had had a good dinner of roast beef, potatoes, and peas and she could not eat any more. Deceased also said that she had had some peasoup on Saturday night, and also some mince-pie on Friday and this had not agreed with her. When she fell she gave a sigh and expired.— Dr. Robinson said he had known Mrs. Waterworth over 30 years. He had professionally attended her. He had seen her several times lately, and had noticed her aging. She did not appear to lose any flesh. She was an excellent midwife, a most reliable woman and was perfectly worthy of anyone's confidence. She was a very careful person. The cause of death was eyncope, caused by flatulent colic.—The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical testimony. FUNERAL Or AN OLD TRADESMAN AT RUNCORN. The funeral of the late Mr. William Stretch, who died at his residence in linntinck-street on Thursday, after a felon illness, took place on Sunday afternoon at the Runcorn cemetery. The cortege left the deceased's late residence in the following order :—Members of the Roae of Sharon Lodge of Oddfellows, the Volunteer band, members of the Runcorn town band (who acted as bearers), the hearse and mourners, including deceased's widow and children, and a number of personal friends. On the route to the cemetery, the Volunteer band (to which the deceased was formerly attached) played the "Dead March." The burial service was condu -Acid by the Rev. 11. B. Firth. The coffin was covered with a number of choice wreaths contributed by the members of the family, the town band, Mr. and Mrs. Raining, Mr. Peter Riley and others. The deceasel was 58 years of age. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. W. Benkes, of High-street. TESTI' Or A WATCM AT RUNCORN: _ _</p>
        <p>It the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday before Mr. S. Taylor, Carl Lyon, a Norwegian sailor, was charged with stealing a watch and chain valued at U.s, from tbe ship called the "Fred" on the 22ad inst. the property of Hans Olsen, also a N )rwegian.—Prisiner was remanded until Thursday to enable the police to make more in- quiries. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>- --- At the Runcorn Police Court on Thursday morning, before Mr. F. J. Norman and Mr. W. Timmins, Carl Lyon. a Norwegian sailor, of the vessel Selina, was charged on remand with stealing a watch and chain, valued at £1 11s. on the 22nd inst. from the ship "Fred" lying at Runcorn Docks, the property of Nana Olsen.— The prosecutor, a Norwegian, could not speak English and Thomas Stevesen a mate on the vessel " Fred " acted as Interpreter. He said he was a seaman on board the" Fred." On Saturday he was employed as the cook and his watch and chain was hung on a nail. About noon he saw the prisoner in the galley. Between 6 and 6.30 o'clock the same day he missed the wat3h and chain. 011 Monday witness gave information to the police. The watch produced was his property.—Thos. Stevesen said he saw Leyon on board their vessel be- tween 12 and 1 o'clock on Saturday.—William Craddock and A. Dodd repeated the evidence they gave on Tues- day.—P.C. Wylie proved arresting the pris mer on the boat "Selina "lying at Runcorn Docks a short distance from the "Fred." On Wednesday be searched the yes- sel and found the watch among a heap of rubbish in the rope locker. The chain had not yet been discovered,— P.C. Gregory said he had seen the prisoner in the town for the last three weeks.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for six weeks witb bard labour.</p>
      </div>
