      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>SI.LUSEMN</p>
        <p>._4</p>
        <p>AND REGULA.RS. HOW TO MAKE THE ARMY A BLESSING.</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday night, at Hartford, Northwich, in pre- sence of a large gathering, Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P., opened a new drill hall, library, billiardroorn. &amp;c., designed for the use of H Company, 2nd Volunteer Battalion Che Mire Regiment. Sir John also distributed the shooting prizes. Be said that during the last few weeks it had become the duty of every politician to read and to think upon military matters. The air was full cf schemes for the increase of our Army, and he was also happy to think, of schemes of reform (cheers). He had been comparing the efficiency of Volunteers, militia, and yeomanry, who only put in a week or a few more drills to the regular soldiers who stuck to their duties 365 days in the year. He asked himself whether there was net a happy medium between these schemes, and he dreamt that some day perhaps a statesman might have courage, in his desire to nv,he the soldier's profession attractive, to propose that men might be soldiers for three months and ' private citizens the remainder of the year. In England there were a number of season trades, each of which had slack times, and he believed it would be found not a ' hardship but a great help if colliers, agricultural labourers, men engaged in building trades, and others occasionally idle, left their ordinary avocations and took to soldiering three months each year. If the young men of this country i 'mad the home army ander such conditions, the- would, instead of being idle, find happy oceicion, and England would thereby obtain a contented and attractive army with a higher moral character, while the cost would be much less, and the forces equally as effi- cient. They would find that when a man had served his three or four years under such conditions there would be very little chance of his becoming a tramp,and every man in all ranks world be absorbed in- to the civil population with as great credit to themselves as happened in the case of Cromwell's Ironsides. In- stead, also as of present of large numbers taking to the road and leading an idle life, they would by providing the men, when it was impossible for them to earn a liv- ing, with a happy occupation make the Army a blessing to the whole of our population (cheers.) Speeches were also delivered by Captain Ingram Thompson, Surgeon- Major Moreton, and Chaplain Eidows, the latter stating that Sir John Brunner bad generously provided the band of that company with their instruments. Sir John now pinned on the breast of ex-Sergeant Johnson the Volunteer long-service medal, and Cc cup finalists pre- sented on his own behalf money prizes. An entertain ment followed.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN fIuAIE MISSIONS. SERVICES AT RUNCORN. The annual services in aid of Wesleyan Home Missions in connection with the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit, were held on Sunday. In the morning at St. Paul's, the Rev. R. J. Clifton preached, and the Rev. C. W. L. Christien occupied the pulpit in the evening. At the Halton-road Chapel, the Rev. C. W. L. Christien preached the morn- ing sermon, and in the evening the Rev. R. J. Clifton preached. The choirs at both churches rendered special hymns. Collections were taken in aid ef the Home Missions. The annual meeting was held on Monday evening in S Mr. A. R. Norman, J.P., presiding. He. was Paul's C e pphourrtceh, by the Revs.d S. H. Hallam of (I.; ver• pool),C. W. L. Christian, R. J. Clifton, J. A. B. Malvern, T. Simister, R. .JR. N. Hor.mpaenen,eJt.tP,., G. Bellamy, and E Taylor. RMaeTwsshirelsn.Rs°Fenv.. R. J. Clifton read the annual report. It showed that the income during the past year had been £42,123 73. 3. and the expenditure £43,001 12s. 61. ' The expenditure exceeded the income by £B7B 53. 3d. Adding this amount to the balance of £1,096 17s. Id. due to the treasurer at fast vsar's audit, there was a deficit on the society of £1,975 2s. It The claims upon the Society were numerous and ur Homegen. TheMyibs Missionary bad home mission cars in complete working order, four of which had been added during the past year at a cost of £4OB 143. 81. Bibles, tracts, to the value of £5OO had been sold in the towns and vil- laees of England by the men in charge of these Cars. There were many other claims upon the fund which could not be enumerated in a brief report. The Runcorn circuit had contributed £49 183. 41 made up in the fol- lowing items :—Schools £5 14s. 61. ; congregation £ll 3s. Be. ; public meeting £l5 17s. ; juvenile associa- tion (Camden branch) £ll 63. 101., and subscriptions £5 163. The Chairman, in the course of his remarks, said he considered this Society was one of the most important and far-reaching in its effect in connection with their church. When they remembered the work that was done a nucleus of which teey had in the report given them loat, and how far-reach- ing he R.J..ae Clifton certain ll a tn t hai t it would have their y the a w Rev.ag. I hearty support. (Applause.) There were eight cars Imoving about tee country, and very largely about the there villages' " was any part of the country more "dden liable to get priest-ri , or to come under the heel of Priest-craft—which he was sorry to say wig much in evidence to-day—it was their villages. It was indeed a very good thin., to have these cars movine, about giving e the people plain gospel and evan ,e 1 cal truth. Mr. Nor- manin c onelusion referred to the contingent fund and appealed for support. The Rev. J. A. B. Malvern, in the course of a short addrees,eaid he considered year by year Christ wee gett- ing a firmer grip of this magnificent country of ours. There was 11 great moral gulf between the past centuries and the present. They had infinitely more of Jesus Christ than they had in the centuries gone by. He spoke of the different kings and queens of England. He com- pared the lives of past kings with the life of Her Grac- ious Majesty the Queen. He also compared the lives of former politicians with the lives oz Lord Salisbury and Mr. Gladstone. The Rev.S.H.Haliam,who wag received with applause, said there were great probleml which still confronted them, and which the Ceurch was bringing to bear on the present day society to win them to Christ. The Church had been misrepresented and misjudged by the people outside. He had heard it said that the church was a friend of the classes and an enemy of the masses, that the preachers dined with the rich and then preached to the poor. Unquestionably there was an attempt made in more places than one to influence the public opium • in in this direction, but he believed a great oppor- tunity was corning for the church to grapple with the democratic spirit of the age, and the question of the hour was who should lead the democracy. His impres- sion was that the working class were to be led by co other than those who professed to call themselves Christians. (Applause.) In con- clusion, the rev. gentleman said the great way to settle the problems of the hour was not by fighting them out, but by praying them out ; not by setting master against man or riches against poverty, as though they were in absolute hostility, but by bringing the parties into line, man to man, and leadingthem to Calvary's Cross. (Loud applause.) On the motion of the B. C. W.L. Christian, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman for pre- siding, and on the motion of the Chairman,seeonded by Mr.Bellamv,a similar compliment was paid to the Revs. S. H. Hallaen'and J. A. R. Malvern, and ',carried unani- mously.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL NOTES. Lest Saturday the ex-charnplons had no fixture to ful- fil and consequently were allowed a rest. Looking down the table of the Northern Union, Runcorn cannot boast of having (lone anything extraordinary, unless it be in the losing line.i For when one looks in that direction ex- traordinary s not the word. It is surprising how the wind of success has blown so fiercely against them. When one takes the team that won championship honours three ycers ago and compares it with the one that is n i now doing duty tis seen that there has not been ge t e men,though certainly there has any great chair . been a great—a very great—change in the style of com- bination, and the play generally. Butterworth's being hurt in the early part of this season, has no doubt tended to weaken the ranks. His re-appearance now, will be hailed with much satisfaction.</p>
        <p>-- Runcsrn have not many More league fixtures to play, and as there is now no room left to them to become famous, they must wait patiently for the cup competi- tions. It is to be hoped that they will do a little better in this competition than they have done in the League Competition, but as they have let one honour slip away, no doubt every member of the team will strain an extra nerve for the honour of the port. Their first encounter is with Civerston at tftverston. If " gossip " had its way Runcorn would come out at the close of the compe- tition at the head of the poll.</p>
        <p>The committee are doing all in their power to put the men in fit and proper condition for the coming struggles. Three meetings are now to h held week y. On Tuesday and Thursday evenings, the Drill Hall is turned into a football field for the time being, and the men in their football costumes practise combination, &amp;c. After the do "on Thursday, they adjourn to Riley's Café, and partake of a good meal. Here the different men's move- ments are diicussecl, and if any have been fAulty in their play they are corrected.</p>
        <p>This is a veil' wise step on the part of the committee, and it will also keep the players in touch with each other. It is a pity that the idea was not sooner brought into practise for there is no telling what migbt have hap- pened with Runcorn's opponents, if the green jersey men had had these performances early in the season.</p>
        <p>This week the CeArians visit the watering place to play the men of Morecambe,who defeated the ryldesley- Res last Saturday. Runcorn's team inc:udes on. or two A team men, who have been shining lights with the junior string. Morecambe have surpris,cl Runcorn once, but I think I know on whose side the biggest score wiil t,e to-morrow.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 28, I‘9B. SIR JOHN BRUNNER ON VOLUNTEERS I SIR JOHN BRUNNER AND HIS CONSTITUENTS. SPEECH ON POLITICAL AFFAIRS. Sir John T. Bruener, Bart., M.P., addressed a meet- ing of his constituents on Monday evening in the Glad- stone Club,Northwich. The chair was occupied by Mr. H. Bratt,and there was a good attendance. The hon. member said that a few nights ago he listened to a speech by his right hon. friend Mr. Asquith, in which there was not a taunt, not a bitter word, in which there was not a phrase nor an idea which could be offensive to his most ietermined and bitter opponent. On the fol- lowing day a speech was delivered by Mr. Chamberlain in the Conservative Club at Liverpool. Mr. Chamberlain alluded to a subject which was under discussion in the House of Commons,:which was the immediate cause of tho fall of the last Government—cord.te—an explosive subject at the time, and the reverberations of its explosion had:root cased entirely yet. Mr. Chamberlain said he fancied that the cordite had entered the soul of Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman. Now, the Colonial Secretary had often said things which there- '1 after were tabooed by men of good taste. Quoting Sir Henry's words—that ne present Government was a Government of bribes and doles—Mr. Chamberlain said it was a rather indelicate remark to make in the presence of the gentleman who was in the chair at the time—a gentleman whe had received a title from the Liberals. He would not answer the statement about cordite, be- cause it would be bad taste, but it was very bad taste on Mr. Chamberlain's part to say what he did about Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman'a chairman. The allegation was that the Government e, as a Government of doles and bribes. On this question of the granting of titles for political services the pot was as black as the kettle every bit. (Ilear,hear.) There was not a pin to choose between the two parties. V:111 equally gave titles as rewards for political services. He approved of the prac- tice. If they could through their institutions secure that services to the country should stand above money, they ..rould do well. There were other parts of the world where motley went to the top and service to the corn- naunity came very far halo w ; but as long as this insti- tution of giving titles for political services, no matter on which side rendered, were rendered by men who heartily desired the good of their country,independently of party, he approved of it. But let them look back at the last Jubilee and the Jubilee before it. Did they not think that the Tory Government of the day, in view of the known enthusiastic loyalty of the people to the Sovereign ought to have banded the whole nation together by discarding political questions at such a time? What was done ? Titles were distributed—hut all to the Tory party. Now, he clan-nod that the Tory party ought on that occasion to have rewarded services to the nation irrespective of party. (Hear,hear.) The charge as to this Government being a Government of doles and bribes was made with very good reason. The whole nation had been taxed tor the benefit of one religious sect, the Church of England. We had given £700,000 a year practically to the Church of Eneland, through the Church Schools, and about £2,000,000 to the landlord Class. A gain,it was proposed to give a dole to the sugar planters of the West Indies. Coming nearer home the bon. member expressed his views in regard to the dis- pute in the engineering trade. Ile declared that he was as great a believer in the principles of trade unions as ever. His view was that so long as trade unionists sought to get as large a share as they possibly could of the profits of an industry they were on right lines, and that they were on wrong lines altogether when they sought to reduce the profits. When they did that, he thought they were adopting a suicidal and an immoral course. Some persons who called themselves Socialists had been teaching the doctaine that the less a man did the more there was left for others to do. That was an entirely mistaken notion. He held that the more a man did, consistent with his leading a happy life, the better for himself and for the whole community.—(Hear hear.) Sir John next uttered a warring note against government by syndicates. It had been alleged,he said, that we had been governed by university men, by mas- ters of arts, and he had advised that there should be a larger infusion of buoinese men into the House of Com- mons. At the same time he bade his hearers beware of government by syndicate, through syndicates, and for the benefit of syndicates, and he suggested that, that great syndicate, and Chartered Company of South Africa had had a good deal to do with the bucking of the in- quiry into the circumstances of the Jameson raid and the conduct of Mr. Cecil Rhodes. Taking up Mr- Chamberlain's remark about the colonies being undeve- loped estates, Sir John said he demurred to spending the money of the Britiih taxpayers for the development of estates which did not belong to us in any real sense. There was an estate at home that needed developing. He believed that if we gave the agriculturist absc lute security of tenure and free access to the land we might produce twice as much from the land as we did, and we should then do better for ourselves than if we had the whole of Africa as a gift. On the subject of local veto he had to say that while be- lieving in the right of localties to determine what drink- ing facilities should exist in their midst, he thought that when next an appeal was made to the electorate the Liberal party must put a handsomer face on their tern- rerance proposals, and call them by a better name than "local veto." An impression seemed to have got abroad at the last general election that the great mai nits, of Liberals were "kill-joys," that they were bent on stop- ping men from enjoying this life.He did net believe that was true of the Liberal party, and so h 3 said that, as practical politicians, they must when next they ap- proached the question of legislation put it handsomer face on their principles, and adopt a better title. Local veto had a frightening effect on the voters, and they threw out the Liberal Government. On anolier occa- sion they must present their principles in such a man- ner as to Abe attractive to the mass of voters In that way they would do a work which would rebound to the credit of the Liberal perty, and to the enormous advantage of the country. (Cheers.) With regard to foreign affairs, he had do hesitation in saying that for two ard a half years the Government had funked as no other British Government had done before. They had funked before Germany, Russia, and other Powers, and now, after all the lashing and kicking they had had from the Liberals, the Government were going to speak out,to leave the Concert of Europe, and to matn'ain our trade with China even at the cost of war. This declara- tion had gratified the whole of the country from one end to the other—that at last they had plucked up their courage. He had seen that this declaration had driven the Liverpool Tories absolutely drunk and delirious with joy, but let him tell them that no credit for it was due to the Tories ; it was the great majority of Liberals who had at last kicked the Government into showing some semblance of fight. (Cheers.) On the motion of Mr. G. King, seconded 137 Mr. W. Verdi°, a vote of thanks was passed to the hon, member for his address. vote, Sir John Brunner alluded negertnhien the health of Mr, Gladstone. Itnheacrkenpoowelsecogin to He had, he said, the pleasure some six months aeo of Pleating Mr. Gladstone in Lord Renders house, near Cannes, and although Mr. Gladstone had not had a mo- ment's comfort for four menth,, owing to the neuraleic paine,yet when a lady came into the room he rose from his chair as quickly as he (Sir John) could. Mr. Glad- stone's voice was as resonant and as full as ever he had heard it,while the brilliancy of his conversation and the marvellous accuracy of his memory were as evident as they had ever been. Since then there had been con- flicting rumours,but according to the latest accounts the pain in the face was somewhat abated, and he believed that we should have Mr. Gladstone back again in this country in as good health as one could expect a man of eighty-eight to be.</p>
      </div>
