      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ENGINEERS' DISPUFE.</head>
        <p>THE ENGINEERS' DISPUFE.</p>
        <p>The ballot of the members of the trade unions upon a the ques'lon of the acceptance of the emended terms of settlement of the engineering dispute was completed on Thursday night. The result is to be reported to a Pint leoxyepresc'teFdedtheraatttiohn e meeting of representatives of the ndE im taenrdmthewill iwore f-m workmen niay ratified.nllLondon Returns available with regard to the votes of the workmen in the Manchester district shaw that 2,193 members of the Amalgamated Socit ty of Engineers voted in favour of the adoption of the terms, while 464 voted against them. The returns have been completed of the ballot of the United Machine Workers' Association. Of the me m bers of this trade union, 1,903 voted in favour and 488 against the terms. Mr. Barnes stated in London on Thursday evening that, roughly speaking, the ac,..eptances to hand were in the proportion of about 25,000 to 11,000,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCRES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCRES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>There was an encouraging attendance at the meeting of this S-ciety on Sunday afternoon, when the proceed- ings were presided over by Mr. William Roberts. In the course of a few observations, Mr. Roberts said he was glad to see the great interest which was taken in Chris- tianity by workingmen, as evidenced at the recent dis- cussion on Christianity and Spiritualistn.Tne address for the afternoon was delivered by Mr. Brown, of Manches- ter, who referred to the mission to the North Sea fisher- men, and gave an interesting account of the work. At the conclusion h.l showed a number of curios whiek he had brought from the North Sea..—Mr. E. Batten,late of Paris, very effectively rendered two solos" PruPeccatis" and "Nazareth." The service was also taken part in by Mr. D. Owen, Mr. William Lowe, and Mr. Farrar.</p>
        <p>WY SUFFER ONE MOSlEN'T—from torturing, dis- figuring skin humours, when a warm bath with CIITICURA SOAP and a single anointing, with Crum 'IRA, the great skin cure, will afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy. permanent, and economical cure, when the best physicians and all other remedies fail. No preparations ever compounded in the history of medicine have performed the wonderful cures daily made by CUTICIJR9. remedies. They are, in truth, the greatest skin cures, b'ood purifiers, and humour remedies of modern times, and appeal to those who have suffered long and hopelessly, with irresistible force.</p>
