      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES IN D</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES IN D</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington were not quite able to inflict the defeat upon Salford which they had fondly imagined themse;ves doing. The Reds came here on Saturday and went away with one of our much-needed points, which makes the third they have robbed Warringtos of in the present season. In the first encounter at New Barns, Salford uncharitably took advantage of Warrington's sadly weakened forces and ran in quite a number of goals and tries,likewise placing about half the players hors de combat. They are funny folks at Salford, and seem to have tempers much impaired by their enforced trudge through the sludge outside the er closure. fhe primrose and blues appeared to be getting hard knocks all that afternoon, and first one and then another would stop to rub an injured limb,with the unlooked for consequence that the sportsmanlike spectators hooted them separate-, ly, individually,and collectively.Finally,Barratt felt him- self so badly used that he limped down the touchline, I andiinstead of sympathy met with a storm of jeers and howls. And after that they rubbed the score in. Pleas- ant wasn't it ! These little incidents caused the players to vow vengeance on Salford,and Saturday was the oc- casion for wiping off old scores. The lapse of time had taken the edge off mast of tke resentment,and for the most part the game was fought out in a friendly fashion. At the close of hostilities each side had scored a try.</p>
        <p>It is said that there are 13 Welshmen in the Salford fifteen. Whether this be quite correct or not, the names of the players themselves indicate that the Principality is strongly represented. Sine libeller in days gone by said that Taffy besides being a Welshman, was a thief, and furthermore overlooked the counter-balancing fact that he can play football better than anyone else. Now the position of affairs is reversed. The prowling com- mitteeman cares not whether Taffy be a thief,murderer, or anything else shady, so long as he still retains the an- cestral footballing genius. For two years there has been an ever-increasing run on the natives of mountainous Wales, and the funny thing is that" stars "from remote colliery villages can give points in football to the trained players of the big Lancashire clubs. There are no Welsh_ men in the Warrington team. All are pure,blue-blooded , Warringtonians, but they don't play better football for "a' that." One of Wales'scienttfic centres would be a much needed acquisition.</p>
        <p>Warrington entered the field minus both Barber ani Boscow,so that the chances of a win were much lessened but the absence of the full-back had is good resu!t in that it took Heesom from centre. There is no glossing over the fact that Heegoso is an absolute failure in the threequarter line, thougn he still plays a good game as custo,lian. There was quite a laugh in the town when it was seen last week that the committee had resurrected Becalm. As matters turned out Cross went centre again, and did rather worse than Heesom might have been expected to do. The game it- self was a most enjoyable one to watch,play fluctuating continually. The teams were constantly on the run,pas- sing was prolific—on the Salford side, and they were evenly matched.</p>
        <p>A decided advantage fell to Warrington at the com- mencement. The choice of positions fell to Bate, and with a strong wind an sun in their favour,the chances of the home team seemed particularly rosy. After the Reds had nearly scored in the first five minutes War- rington assumed the aggressive, and to a large extent maintained it. Every now and then the Salford backs would get possession, and race to the Warrington line, the ball being promptly sent back to the opposite end. Chances went begging on both sides for a time, but at last an impression was made on the Salford defence. The f Irwards—it's always the forwards—got the oval among them,and Taylor picking up gave Deakin a nice pass, with the resalt that the ball was planted over the border. Cross was given a shot at goal,but failed as usual. The man who kicks the next goal for Warrington de- serves a leather medal.A few other chances were thrown away before half time, when Warrington held the upper hand by a try to nil.</p>
        <p>Bate showed good general-ship on burning round, by taking Mason from the scrimmage and playing 5 three- quarters. With tho wind in their favour the speedy Salford backs would soon have made rings round the Warrington quartette,but the five prompt tacklers broke up their combination very effectively. Salford were much the heavier in the forward ranks,so that it speaks well for the energies of the home forwards that they could, and did, hold their own with 7 in the pack. I don't think there is a better all-round set of scrim- magers in the League than the Warrington lot. They work hard, heel out quickly, and in the loose dribble in a fashion which nonplusses their opponents.</p>
        <p>A determined rush by the home team signalised the re-commencement of hostilities, and the Salford line was quickly endangered. Cross let his side down, and Sal- ford put on the screw. Hoskins nearly equalised, and then a score came when not expected. A rather lucky rebound of the ball off Griffiths enabled the little ball back to score far out. He made a fine attempt at goal, and then all was level pegging. Truth to tell Salford had the best of it and continually made efforts to repeat the performance. There was no rounding the lengthy home thrreq 'after lino however, and frequently enough Warrington raced to the opposite end. Just before the close hfoores got clear away, only to lose the ball when near the line. The result was a fair indication of the play, though it is improbable that Salford would have scored but for Griffiths' piece of luck combined with smartness. Duckworth was not quite blameless.</p>
        <p>The home spectators were anxious to see the Salford right wing at work. Williams and Hoskins have very nearly,if not quite,established a record for one wing, in the scoring line,and ni,arly every match they have 83- cured a try each. They did not cross the Warrington line last Saturday and neither played quite as brilliantly as I have seen them do. Nevertheless che way in which Williams wormed his way through the home backs in order to give his partner an opening was a treat. He plays the true centre game, and passes like a machine. A man of his ability would increase the scor- ing power of Warrington by at least half. As things are at present, the forwards do most of the scoring,which is a distinct reflection on the capabilities of the backs. With such scientific teams As Salford it is otherwise. On Wednesday they played Rochdale Hornets and won by a goat and 4 tries to a goal and 3 tries. All the tries scord by Salford were secured by the backs—Williams, Pearson, Griffiths, and Gray, one each. That shows the I superiority of their system very clearly.</p>
        <p>The four half-backs On the field last Saturday were well-matched, and played a clever game, Moores and Isherwood were far ahead of Hallam and Crnsg, in the home threequarter line,while among the forwards. Tay- lor,Deakin,and Mason. seemed most prominent. Heesom played well, and is still a cap kble reserve fullback. On the Salford side Harter and Pearson were much below their confreres on the right wing. Smith got through plenty of work at the back, and in the front ramk j. Williams, Tunney, and Moss were frequently to the fare.</p>
        <p>The results in the Lancashire League up tl date are as follows : Avg,.</p>
        <p>For</p>
        <p>P. W. L. 16.-d. T. P. UP. P. P Oldham 19...17... 1... 1-22 55-209...13 14- 68...35 Swinton l7 .15. 1... 1...22 62-233...12 7- 45...31 Widnes 18...13... 4... 1...26 51-205... 8 19- 73...27 Salford 21...12... 7... 2...23 59-223...21 37-153...26 Broughcoa Rgrs 17...11... 4 .. 2.-26 35-157... 9 16- 66...24 21. 9. 10.- 2...13 31--119...21 32-138...20 Warrington 18. 8...10_ 0...17 26-112...19 27-119...16 Runcorn 20- 91..17 32-130-16 ' Wigan 18. 7.-11... 0...13 22- 92...25 33-149...14 St Helens 19.. 7...1E... 0...11 20- 82...22 53- 203...14 Tyldesley 18. 7...10... 1...24 28-132...19 34-140 ..13 *Leifin lg. 5_13.- 0...17 22-1a0...23 47-187...10 S:ockport Rochia'e Wrnetsl9... 4.. 15... 0...19 20- 98...21 49-195 8 3...16... 0 -12 10- 54...27 6)-234... 6 Moerraeigm hbh°,,v, had two points deducted for playin4 an in- eligible man. The most surprising result on Saturday was the defeat of Tyldesley by Morecambe, and on th 3 ground of the former. and the greatne-s of the feat is added to by the fact that Tyldesley were likewise defeated at More- cambe. Well done! seasiders ! With Rochdale flomets in poor form there is just a chance after all of the sea- siders escaping the wooden spoon. Anyhow the struggle at the bottom end of the table promises to be quite as exciting as the struggle for premier honour, s' there is little reason to fear that the competition will flag,</p>
        <p>Both Oldham and Swinton got through their engage- ments on Saturday successfully,but there was a distinct difference in the manner in which their points were . gained. Thus against the moderate Stockport combina- tion the leaders were actually five pointa behind at half time. Had the Cheshiremen not collapsed completely in the later stages Oldham should have been defeated. This following on the matchea against Warrington and Widnes points to something being iad,cally wrong with the Oldham defence, which ma 3: easily ems them dear. Swinton on the othi-r hand continue to go great gunstand a glance at their scoring average clearly shows the re- markable all round strength. of the team. Leigh went down before them by 26 points to two. Swinton have some stiff matches to get through yet,notably Broughton Rangers this week rod, Cldham on February 19:-.1], and Widnes twice. The match between St. Helene and Wigan ended in a win for the former by 9 points to 8 Traynor did all the scoring for the winners, and had also a goal and two tries disallowed.</p>
        <p>stmary's gave the powerful Widnes "A" team a good tame. 89 their defeat by only a goal and a try shows. Play had as much of the game as their opponents, and had the hardest of luck in not scoring. With all the poaching from their ranks St. Mary's can still give a good account of themselves, and stand a good chance in the third division. They are only three points behind the leaders, Whitworth,and have 3 matches to play.</p>
        <p>In the 3rd division only two ma.,ches were played on Saturday. Werneth and Whitworth and Todmorcien V. Leigh Shamrocks. Whitworth beat Werneth by 12 points to 2, and Todmorden beat the Shamrocks by 3 plats to 2. During the week. Whitworth have had 2 points de- ducted for playing au ineligible man.</p>
