      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BEFOE,E Pith ISTKATM.</head>
        <p>BEFOE,E Pith ISTKATM.</p>
        <p>A HUSBAND'S DRUNKEN VIOLENCE.— USING THE KNIFE. Before Mr. F. Monks and a full bench of magistrates, at the Warrington Police Court on Monday, a respect- able-looking man named Patrick Callinane, of 53, Sharp- str-er, was charged with committing an aggravated as- Sarlt en his wife, Emily Cullinane, by stabbing her with a knife.-13e pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable ex- plained that owing to the slightness of complainant's in- juries and from other causes he had not preferred a charge of wounding against prisensr. The parties had beeu married tour or five years and had no children. Latterly they had been rather unhappy together. On Saturday night prisoner mace an appointment with his wife in town, but she failed to meet him. At 11.20 he came home muddled with drink and upbraided her, and finally said "I am going to stab you and Rill my- self afterwards." She was sitting with her arms folded and prisoner added " Take your arms away so that I can stab you." He pieked up a knife from the table and lunged at ber, with the result that she received a wound in the elbow. Prisoner then rather aggravated the affair by taking up a larger knife and saying " I mean to kill you now." However, he offered her no further violence and when charged with the offence he said " I don't know anything about it. I must have done it." This morning he was very penitent and promised never to drink again.—Mrs. Cuilinane,whe produced an apron covered with blood, supported the Chief Constable's Statement. She stated that after prisoner had wounded her he was sorry and wanted to dress the wound. She did Ent think he had meant to do her any serious injury. —Be vas committed to gaol tor a month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>•-• HOME SWEET HOME" DEGRADED.</head>
        <p>•-• HOME SWEET HOME" DEGRADED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Tuesday, before Messrs. R. Garnett and T. Birchall,Margaret Collins, 12, Union-street,was charged with neglecting her 5 children in a manner likely to cause than suffering on January 14th and during the past six months.—She pleaded guilty.—lnspector M'Ateer nrosecated and said the de- fendant was the wife ef Stephen Collins, a bricksetter's labourer. She was the mother of five children in age ranging from 11 years to three months. The husband had also been charged bu'. he had absconded. In consequece of complaints on the 3rd of this month witness went to defendant's house and found her with the throe youngest children in the kitchen. They were all very miserable and poorly clad. In the bedroorn, which was very filthy, witness (who was accompanied by P.C. Gibbs), found two of the boys, both of them quite naked, and their bodies actually eaten alive with vermin. Mrs. Collirs said sh er.uld not account for the condition of the family. Witness called again on January 24 and found defendant and the children in the same condition. This was the third time defendant had been prosecuted. Twice she lald been let off on promis- ing to go to the workhouse, but she would not remain there. Her husband had had three months on the same charge. Defendant had gone from bad to worse.—Mrs. Collins offered no explanation of the neglect, and was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour, the children in the meantimo te go to the workhouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>FiamAY.—Bef ore the Mayor (chairman). Councillor Smet burst. Councillor Roberta. DEITYKENNESS.—Oriffiths Thomas Hughes, 47 Bold-st., off Chorlton-road. Manchester, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Friars Green at nine o'clock on Thursday night.—P.C. Bradley said that defendant was drunk and fol- lowing a young lady about. She complained to witness about it, and he had to lock defendent uo—He 'was fined ss. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.— William Griffiths, 93 Bolton-road; Ashton-in-Makerfield, was charged with cruelly illtreating a bone by working it in an unfit state on January :2th —ln- spector Hreatrea Prosecuted and said that at 11 50 on Janu- ary 12th he was in Queen-st. when he saw defendant in charge of three horses attached to an omnibus. He examined one and found it aged and suffering from en old wound on the offside shoulder, which was swollen, Intl ,med. and painful. Defendant said he had driven it from Ashton. and put it in a Delahay said that at 12 20 on the same date he examined the horse In a stable in Queen-st., and found on its shoulder an old wound about the size of a 2s piece The Collar had blood up, n it.—Defendant said the horse had been resting (oar weeks and was all right when he sterted out.— He called a man named Waterwor h, the ostler at Ashton, and e said the same thing.—A fine of 103. and costs (CI Is 6d.) was imposed. A NEIGABOUR's QUARREL —Mary Dolan was charged with assaulting Michael Quinn in Berry's-yard on January 15th.—She pleaded not guilty. —Michael Quinn stated that because he Interfered between defendant and an old woman whom she was beating she hit him in the face with a quart bottle.—Mrs. Quinn corroborated —Defendant called avid. erica to prove that Quinn kicked her severely and pulled a lot of her hair out, and that she did not strike him with the bottle.—The magistrates dismissed the ca,e. M/SBEHATIOUR IN Coturr. —Robert Martin was charged with using profane language outside the Court at 10 10 —tie had previously been summoned for arrears in the mainten- ance of his son in an asylum, and had then behaved in an Impudent manner.—lnspector Sphaks said that when leaving the court defendant used very profane languege about the magistrates, and was at once arrested,—P.C. Bradley corro- borated.—Defendant who still maintained a defiant attitude was severely reprimanded and fined ss. or 7 days in default —He replied "I shan't pay." SATURDAY —Before Mr. Geddes (in the chair), and Messrs. T. Birchen, J. W. Hayward and R. T. Fairolough. ALLEGED THEFT.—Walter WarburtoU, 7, Antrobus-yard, a labourer, was charged with being found in possession of a white silk pocket handkerchief on Friday, and railing to give a satisfactory account of the same —Th,- Chief Corot ible said priamer was present at a sale at Moss Farm, GraPPeu- ball, when an accident took place owinir to one or the cows becoming wild. When the affair h 'opened prisoner ran to a Lady and asked her for a bandage to bind the injured man's wounds. She handed him th handkerchief produced but Instead of using it In that way prisoner kept it for himself.— Detective Inspector Monaghan and Detective Foster found prisoner in a public-house on loriday, and took him into Oustody.—As the lady declined to prosecute the cue was dismissed.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.--Before Mr. P. 'Monks (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Alderman filankhu-st Mr. T. Mohan, Mr. d' W. Hayward, Councillor J thaw Green. and Councillor Smethurst, Mum( D DISORDERLY.—John Skeldon, no address, was , charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street at 5-15 on Saturday night, and pleading guilty, WAII lined be. and costs. D guar EgSTEBO, ASSAULT, AND ATTEMPTED Rkannis Patrick G-ay,ll,Arpley Walk was charg -id with being drunk and disorderly in Arpley Walk, on daturdAy niihii„ and also with assaulting his wife, Jemirna Gray,—He admitted the assault.--Jo Atilfeown was charged with wilfully obstruct- ing the pGlice by attempting to escue Gray.—V.C. Clough saki that at IC-10 on Saturday night he was in Fri,.rs' Green and saw Gray drunk, and 'tailing out" with his wife. His wife complained of his conduct and t fray • ben raised lale fist and knocked her to the ground with a blow In the fans. She was temporarily unconscious and he was going to kick her had not witness pushed him away. Gray then wanted to fight witness, who seized kim, and M'Keuvin with the assist- ance of others rescued him.Wirness blew his whistle and P C's Dtlahay and Bewey came up,and they eventually arrested both trisoners.— Mrs. Gray corroborated and delenslant was "nvieted of an aggravated arUlt and sent to gaol for two CARTER'S LITTLE</p>
