      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>menths.—At the conclncion of the other business, Mrs. Gray asked for a judicial separation from her husband. She said he had threatened her with a razor, and illtreated her fre- quently during the past six months.—The bench granted the separation and ordered defendant to pay 6s. a week for his wife's maintenance, I Two BAD Citanacrxns.—George and James Hopk'ns, brothers, were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Cairo-street, at a q rarter to four on Saturday afternoon; James Hopkins was further charged with doing wilful damage to a water-closet in the Police Station to the extent of El. P.C. Turner said that he received information of a fight taking place in Cairo- street. and found the two prisoners there. They were drunk I and fighting. and appealed to him to let them have their tight out. Witness brought them to the Police Station. P.C. Condllffe said that James Hopkins was placed in No. 1 cell, which was in good order. Next morning witness found the basin of the water-closet broken to pieces. Prisoner denied any knowledge of the basin, though he was the only occup int Of the cell.— The Chief Constable said prisoners had only come out of gaol on January 20th, from doing a month. James had been up 26 times and George 42 times.—The Bench sent Georga to gaol for a month and James for two months, both with hard labour. MgORDERLYNEBB.—WiIIiam Presto's, 6 Heath-lane, was charged with being disorderly in Scotland-road. on January 15th.—He pleaded not guiltv.—P.C. Metcalfe said he had had to eject prisoner from the Scotland-road Theatre, as he was drunk, and he tried to enter four times after. Witness reported him.—He was bound over to keep the peace for six I months and pay 7s. costs. STREET FOOTRALLERS.—Joseph Oakes, Thomas Garry, Samuel Dean, Joseph Howard, and William Howard were charged with playing football in Birchall-street,on January 15, to the annoyance of passengers.—P.C. Carter proved the case against defendants, who were all over 16 years M age. Oakes had been up previously for the same offence.—The magistrates thought defendants ought to have an example made a them, as they had been previously asked to desist as a lady in the street was seriously ill.—They were all severely admonished and fined ss. each.—The Chief Con- stable said they intended to put down football with a strong hand, especially on Sundays. WILFUL WINDOW BREAKING.—JOIDI Travis was charged with wilful damage to a window at 4. Morris's Court on De- cember llth, 1897.—He pleaded guilty.—Mr, Bond the owner of the propetty deposed that he Issu.2d a summons against defendant for breaking the window on the ante named, but he did not appear. The damage was Bs.—Defendant, who had a bad character, was sent to gaol for 14 clays hard labour. BEGGIYG.—WaIter Chadwick. no Address, was charged with begging in 131.1(1ga-street. on Sunday night.—P.C. Delatiay proved the case, and said defendant was pretending to be lame, and when one leg Was ti red he stood on the other,—The Bench sent him to gaol for 14 days.</p>
        <p>TIJESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Mr. T.</p>
        <p>, Birchen. A CABMAN'S Com. THErr.—John Callaghan, 64 John-st., w char ;e1 with stealing from the stable yard or Edward Connor, Sankey•street, at 10 am. on Monday, a macintosh knee-rug value lOs 6d.—Prisoner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constab e said that pri•onar had been in Mr. Connors em- ploy for nine months, but he had recently given way to habits of intoxication. Mr. Connor discharged him a week ago but cn his promising to amend took him ors again. For three days he behay,cl all right but on Monday morning was again discharged for drunkenness. Alter he had gone a knee rug was mirised from one of the landaus in the yard. About ll a.m. on Monday he turned up at the Central Station cab stand and went to a young ratan named Bowen, also in Mr. °minor's employ and offered to sell him the rug for 3s. 6d. Bowen suspected nothing and tried to borrow the money to buy the rug from Mr. Connor himself. Mr. Connor recog- nised it as his and prisoner was given into custedy.—Pri- loner, who seemed repentant, and pleaded having had too much drink, was lined 20s. Inclu,ling costs. . . .</p>
        <p>DEtrxit AND DISORDERLT.—Thomas Burke for being drunk and disorderly in Bold-st. at five minutes to 11 on Monday nigh', was fined Si. and costs or seven days. SCHOOL OASES.—The following persons were dealt with for neglecting to send their children regularly to sehool.—Mr.W. Jones prosecuted :—Joseph Clough, 11 Derby-st.. labourer, fined is. ; Mien Wilkinson, 41 Hoyle-st., engine driver, Is ; Martin Brett, Shepherds Now, labourer, 2s. 6d. ; William 11111.1 Friars Lane,labourer, Is. ; William Bennett, 12 Garden Row. cab driver, Is. ; Abraham Whiteley 23 Dutcon-st wire drawer. 2s. 63.; George Forster, 27 Napier-st , tanner, 2s. Cl. ; James Wile.ck, 40 New Road, labourer, is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEWTON PE IVY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PE IVY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Tatsusnar.—Beore Mr. T. j. Gillespie, Colonel Wood, Mr. C. F. Borron, and Mr. R. Stone. DRUNK AND D/SORDERLIt.—The following were di alt with for these offences Frederick Hill on the 25th January at Newton, 21. 6d. and oosta ; James Downs. on tho and January at Newton. ss. and costs; Peter noughton, On the 22nd January. at Haydock 2s. 6d and costs. DRUNICENNEES.—For being drunk on the 24th January at Wiuwick, Patrick Hussy, was fined ss. and costs ; and John Crabtree, for the same once at Havdock, on the 22nd Janu try. 2s. 61. and costs. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Mary Barnett, plesded guilty to having at Newton, on the 17th January, used obscene language, and was fined Sc. and costs' LICENSING.—Rdward Rogers applied for, and was grante tbetransfer of the license of the Legh Arms, Goibi,ne, in lieu of Henry btorror. PIT-11 AND Toss —William Taylor, of Triarlestown, was charged with haying on tTie 15th January heed playing at pitch and toss.—P.O. Benson stated that on the date named he saw the defendant who was in company with two boys playing at pitch and toss for pennies in Back Houghton-et,— The defend silt said he had no money at the time and could not have been gaming for peilnies. —He was fine]. is. and costs.</p>
        <p>LINMUZZLED Dowd—For allowing their dogs to be at large unmuzzled, the following were fined: Hugh Pritchard. oe the .19th inst. at Newton, 3s. 61. including costs; William Mellows, on the Bth inst. at Barlestowu. 3s, 6d. including costs ; and John Austin, on the 16th inst. at Cross-lane. Harlestown. 39.131. !minding costs. Coerticrrea EVIDENCE.—MichaeI Fagan appeared In answer to a Summons charging him with hevlng been drunk and disorderly at Earlestown en the 22n i inst. The de- fendant who pleaded not guilty was represented by Mr. Su art.—P.C. Aspilen gave evidence as to seeing the defend- ant drank In possession of a gun near the Market Square, Earlestown, about midnight. P.C. Duncan also gave evi- dence in snpport of the charge Questioned by Mr. Stuart as to whether they knew the defendant, both officers replied In the affirmative, and stated that the defendant gave the name of Michel Fagan.—Mr. Stuart said the case was one of mistaken identity. His instructions were that the defendant was not Michael Fagaa. and that he had never is d he was. The defendant was never sooken to by the police, and, at the time mentioned by the officer the defendant was at home in bed. He lodged e ith Mr Price.whe was a respectableihousehelder near the Market Square. tinder the circumstances he would ask the magis- trates, after hearing the evidence, to discharge the defendant —Fagan was then called, and stated that his name was James and not Michael. He hid never told a police officer that his name was Michael, He wa, on the Market Squire on Sunday night, but he went to ',eel at his lodgings (Mr. Price's) aboat eleven o'clock. He did not get up afterwards. and he never saw a constable.—Ry the Chairman : Had yeu a gun in your hand on Sund ty night -No Sin—Have you a gun at all e— Mr. Price has one. but I never used It.—ln answer to Superintendent Hlgginboidorn. witness said be bad a brother named Michael, but he was not living at Earlestown. He did not see any constables on the night ie question. He never saw four constables.—Mr. Stuart called for a witness named Price with whom pagan lived, but he was not present. in Court.—Superintendent Higginbottom asted permission to call two further wirnesses.—Mr. Stuart objected and said as his witness</p>
        <p>_ was not present, the better ouraeto,ajout wou d be to adjourn tha wise until the next Court, and the Superinten- dent could then call his additional wttnesses.—This course Woe agreed to, and the ensa aecor.tingly adjourned.—Micheal Cunneely Was aso charged with being drunk and disorderly at Harlestewn on the 2?.n.1 of danuary.—Mr. Stuart reor sented the defendant who pleaded not guilty.— P.O. Clarke stated that. betweei • Leven and twelve o',•lock on the night of the date named the cle,,ndaet was drunk and causing a disturbancs in Legliest.. BA rirs own. The defend- ant was using disgusting laneusge, and the rilstdrbence caused a crowd to assemble. The defendant said no constab e was able to lock him up. and his eondu,t was sueh that they were obliged to convey him to the p .hce station. - CUM- examined by Mr. .4tuart witne,• denied that any police officer kicked a door in.—The Chairman : Was this man using bad language before or after he we. loaned UP? —Witness; Both b.rfore and after. P.O. Schofield also gave evidence as to the defendant causing a di.turb- ance. -P.O. Brindle stated that he was in coinpiny with the last witness when he heard a great noise, and'. crash of pots at the corner of Legh-street. He saw the defencimt thrown out or a house into the gutter. He commenced *warring and threatened to kick the door of the house in. Witness requested the man to go home but be would not, and they were obliged to lock him up.—Sergeant Stanek, said he Was at the police station when defendent was b-ought la. He was mad drunk and witness ordered him to be placed in the cells.—For the defence, Mr Stuart slated that the d-fendant had been on a visit to a friend named Fleming, and about 11 o'clock he left the house on good friends with everybody. As 'con as he got off the doors'ep he was taken into custody by a police oflieer. and because he resented such conduct he was locked up. There was no disturbance at all, awl he would be able to trove that the defendant was not brown out, of the house, and WAS not drunk at the thus Edward Fleming. Sankey-strect, Barlestewn. deposed that the defendant visited his house on the date inentioned. When he left he was sober, and it wss untrue to say Hid he Was turned out. They parted friends. He saw a constable look In at the door, and the noise mentioned as being heard was due to his daughter breaking the glass chimney of the lamp. —Mrs. Fleming, James Cos.ello and the defendant also gave evidence, In answer to Superintendent lligginbottom, the defendant admitted that he had three glacses of beer, but dented that he was drunks—The Chairman said the magis- trates hadn't the slightest doubt that the defendant was drunk and used bad language. He would be fined Se. and costs.</p>
        <p>WIPE DESERTION.— Samuel Shepherd was charged with deserting hie wife. Martha Shepherd. on the 30th November. Mr. Stuart represented thd ccommi neut. and stated that after the evidence had been given the Case was adjourned from the coact in December at the engraft nof the magistrates, in order that Mr. Harrison, the Police Court Missionary, might make an effort to get the two parties to live together again. He (Mr. Stuart) had seen Mr. Harrison who told him that he had failed to reconcile the two Parties. Mrs. Shep- herd had been to aspull where the defendant worked. and found that he had not taken a house, that he had no furni- ture. and he refused to make any allowance. Mrs. Shepherd who stated that she had been married to the defendant for about 16 years. gave evidence in support of Air. Stuart's statement He was in a rare good situation, and earning about £2 a week.—The defeoclant cal ad two witnesses named Mrs Howarth. and Mrs. Wright. of Harlestown, to speak to the conduct of his wife, when he was away front home, but the Clerk said they proved nothing of a serious cbaracter.—The magistrates ordered the defendant to pay 105- a week to his wife.—On leaving the boa he said" I shall not pay a copper ; I will go down first."</p>
      </div>
