      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>TH E EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 28 3898.</head>
        <p>TH E EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 28 3898.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>TITRHDLY.--E;fo-re Mr.Henry-Wield (in the chair), Captain Rodger. and Messra. H. Talbot, J. Mclntyre, J. Tinsley, and C. H. Broadbent. ALLOWING HORSES TO STRA.Y.—lhOrriall Ba I, Acton Grange, was fined Is. 6d. and coits for a lowing his horses to stray on the highway on December 7th. DRUNKENNESS. -Edward Gal'owty, tbdriver, Warrington. was fined in the coits (35. 61.) for tp.ing drunk at Daresbury, on the 28th December.—For a like off once, Dominick Knock- ton, Grappenhall. Has fined 5s DRIVING WITHOUT LlGurrs.—James Atherton, Farrell- street, Warrington, was fined 2s. 6d. for driving his vehicle without a light on the 30th December at Appleton—For like oßences, George Anemic. Salford. on the 4th January at Acton Grange, was fined Is., and John Morrisey, Warring- ton. ss.</p>
        <p>CARELESS DRlVEFte.—William Smith. Stockton Heath, was charged with not having proper control over his horse an4l lorry at Stockton Heath. on the let Januarv.—Sergeant Gosling Droved the case, and defendant vies fined 15s. in- cluding coats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>FRODSEIAM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FRODSEIAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Reynolds. and Mr. A. Thomas. SAVAGE ASSAULT AT FRODSHAM.—Mary Jane ShOre.married woman, Main-street, Frodsham, was summoned by Mary Alice Bate, for assault.—The comelainant aid on the night of the 21st December, between 7.30 and 8 o'clock, she went to the defendant's house with a dress piece in parcel. Witness expected to receive payment for her work as soon as she handed the parcel over to Mrs. Shore. Defendant said she could not pay and witness remarked that she must either have the money or take the goods back. Mrs. Shore then ran against the door to stop her from getting out. Defendant attempted to tlke the parcel from her and a struggle ensued. Mrs. Shore hit her on the face and cut her imp. Bel ore witness could get into the street defendant again struck her on the head. Witness then went to the Police Station —Martha Rogers, said Miss Bate left her house about seven o'clock. and when she returned her face was covered with blood. She was scarred very much, and her clothes bad been torn almost off her back.—Sergeant Clays spoke to the condition of complainant when she called at the Police Station en the night in questi o . —The defendant said complainant kmeked her child who had just recovered from a serious illness. She never hit the complainant at all. She did not want complain- ant to take the goods away. She intended to pay her in a very short time. Complainant hit her (defendant) first and she in turn pushed her.—Annie Shore. defendant's daughter, said her mother took the parcel off Miss Bate but she did not bit her.—The Bench thought the case proved and said it was a most disgraceful assault. If she (olefendant) had not the money to pay for the things she should have allowed the complainant to take her goods ,way. A fia.e of 10s. and 15s. 8.1. costs was imposed, or 14 days with hard labour. The money was paid. • .</p>
        <p>• ',- DRUNK AND .14oRDERLT.—The following persons were fined for this offence : Fred Garner at Frodsham Lordship, on 28th or December, ss. and Bs. 6d. costs; :arsenal Brown at Frodsham Lordship. on 15th of January. 10s. and costs ; George Ainsworth at F•ocishain on 15th of January, ss. and Bs. 6d. costs ; and Williana Baton Dykes at Frodsham Lord ship on 15th January, ss. and Bs. 6d. amts. bTRAY Cows.—Mary Nixon was fined ss. including costs for allowing certain cows ta stray on the highway at Helsby o* the 28th December—P.C. Needham proved the ease..</p>
        <p>No Wpm—Thomas Wright, farmer, Was fined ss. includ- ing costs for not having a lighted lamp attached to his vehicle on the 18th January at Alvanley. _ .</p>
        <p>CONTRAVENTION OF THE PITAIC HEALTH Ac—Thomas Aston was charg.d with occupying a house contrary to section VI. of the Public Health Act (water) 1.878, at Frodsham Lordship on the 25th December. —James Farrington, nuis- ance Inspector prosecuted on behalf of the Runcorn Rural District Council. As the defendant appeared to be ignorant of the law, the inspector did not press the case and he was lined 6d. and Is. 6d. costs.</p>
        <p>DAMAGING A TURNSTlLE,—Piederick Wharton WAS BUM- monad for dAng wilful damage to a turnstile at Frodsharn Lordship, on the 23rd January.—Thomas Ainsworth said on Sunday morning last he saw the defendant and three other youths going up the path-walk. The others walked through the turnstile, but the defendant took hold of it and pitched It up tke fiel I.—Defendant said the style was loose and when he placed his hand on it, it fell over. William Webb said there was nothing to keep the sti'e together, anyone could knock it over.—t) tendant was fined 6d. and 123. 61. casts. or 14 days with hard labour in default.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Moxnar.—Before Major flazlehurst and Mr. Philip Speakman. TEMPoRI.II2 AUTHoUIFF.—John Winfield was granted temporary authority to sell at the Railway Hotel, Lowland's - road. Similar authority %vat granted to William Cobley, to sell at the Frieniship Tavern, Fryer street. A Boaxatax FINED FOR CRUELTY% —James Moore, boat- man. was su nmoned for cru•ily illtre sting a horse by work- log the same whilst in an unfit state, at Runcorn. on the 20th January.—lnspe2tOr Grettrex of the E.8.P.0.A., stited th it he was on duty on the canal bank on the date ntmed when he saw the defendant In charge of a brown gelding The animal had just been released from the boat '• Alfred., ' lb was very lame and he cashed the defendant's attention to It. Dfl evident said it was lame when he bought It. On ex- amination, witness found that it was an aged brovla gelding, and was suffering from a diseased back and fetlock joint Moore admitted that he had driven the horse from Btockton Heath Bridge near Warrington, with a load of 60 tons of slack.—Gefendant was fined ss. and Costa. DRUNK AND Die silLEßLY.—Arthur Dyer, el Waterloo Hotel, Runcorn, was fined 10s. including Costs for being drunk and disorderly in Duke-street, Runcora, on the 16th January. P.O Hu.dies proved the oase.—Robert McKeown, seaman.was tined lOs. inelud;ng costs for being drunk and disorderly in Regent-street on Saturday night.—For a similar offence Patrick Meakin. labouree, was also fined 10s. Including costs. A DES itRTER SURRENDERS.—Thomas Therap9oll way charg-.d with being a deserter from the Gordon Highlanders on the 28th January, 1897.—P.0. Hughes abated that the prisoner enter ei the Police Station and said he wished to surrender as a deserter from the Gordon riLlalanders. Witness consulted" Po:lce Gazette," and found the state- ment of the prisoner was correct. He was described as being a deserter from the regiment en route from Glasgow to Aldershot. Ttit Denoo remarried prisoner to Knutsford Gaol to await an escort</p>
        <p>SCHOOT, ATriND ',NO F 1 CASES. —TtiOnUll StokeB. Church- street; William Co .ice. Surre-street; ruornas Jones. Surrey- stre.t ; Thorne, Harding. Shaw-street were etch fined te. Including costs for neglecting to send their children to school.</p>
