        <p>AN OLD Womart'fi-FATAL FosoNEsS Fort 1 OBACCO. Mr. F. A. Jones. Gputy coroner, held an inquest tbt the o:'accident, o f workhousetarGoinlywint,d(slBosydeaayrsnoi:hatgae,B at the Workhouse on Saturday atternoon ou the our last issue. Patrick Hanlon,who was lodging at the deceased's honse in Friars'-green at the time of wreh:;odrtieedd at deceased was smoking her pipe. He saw her clothes Paid on August 10th, at midday, on fire, and he put nut the filmes. He did not see how they got on Le, but he supposed that the paper with which she was lighting her pipe must have dropped on her after she had thrown it away. —A juryman : Was she an invalid ?—Witnez,s She complained of rheumatism, and could not walk very well.---clatherine Hanlon, daughter-in-law. said the deceased was taken to the Infirmary, where she was attended to by Dr. Battle.—The evidence of nurses at the Union showed that deceased was admitted there on the 12th October suffering from burns, and died on January 19th.—A verdict of " Accidental death "was returned. Horir Timgrrir BRAters, G.F.S. On Monday evening an entertainment in eoeneCtion with the above branch was held in the schoolroom, Museum-street, the Vicar, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton in the chair. There was a large attendance and the I I proeeeds will be idevoted to the Girls' Friendly Society Sick Fun&amp; The programme was as : —Glee, members nf the G.F.S., taught by Mrs. R. Johnson; violin solo,Mr. Felix Berlyn ( Manchester); song, Mies F. Rigby, k .0 F'S,; "Minuet dais Coot," Misses E. Young and R. Wellhead ; piano solo, Miss Haring (Manchester); mandoline solo, Miss E. Tun- stall, accompanist Miss M.Jonss ; song Mrs. R. Johnson ; violin solo, Mr. Felix Berlyn ; song, Mr. Eccles; mandoline solo. Miss E. Tunstall ; piano solo, Mies Harinkt •, M.G.F S., children's voices. The !members of the G.F.S. who contributed the glee had evidently been carefully trained by Mrs. R. John- sen, and Mr. Berlyn was enthusiastically applauded for his fine performances on the violin. Miss llar- ing is an accomplished pianist, and Mrs. R. Johnson had the honour of a recall for her excellent render- ing of the song, "Come sweet morning." In res- ponse she gave "The First 'Violet," by Mendelssohn. , Mr. Eccles was also heard to great advantage.</p>
      </div>
