      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WIDNES v. f.HELENS RECS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. f.HELENS RECS.</p>
        <p>The Widat s team engaged in a friendly encounter with the Rees on Saturday.at the Lowerhouse enclosure. There was a very poor attendance.Widnes won the toss, and Brennan elected to play with a fairly strong wind in his favour. The visitors started,and by smart work, Widnes got to the Rees' quarters, where they were allowed a penalty, but nothing was gained by the kick. Peters suceeeded in clearing smartly, but the referee failed to see him put his men on-side. Johnson had a shot at goal from a penalty,but nothing was gained,and some injudicious kicking sent the ball dead on two occasions.. Taylor got etWAV nicely, but his pass was not taken advantage of. A smart run by Hughes brouglst relief to the Rees' but Johnson gained possession and dashed over witha try. Batey landed a splendid goal from a somewhat difficult position. From the centre kick tise Rees rushed up to the home half, but Batey was instrumental in saving. Donnolley kicked well for Widnes, Crassley making his mark, and returning through Siddall. Tne visitors attacked. but Murphy kicked clear, and halfway was again the scene of operatims. Hughes got away for the visitors. Cr 3ssley was of great service to his side, and several times repulsed the home team. Archer got away and kicked to touch near the Widnes line, where the " Chemicals" were penalized. Bidden made two at- tempts to land a goal but failed. Half-time arrived with the score as follows :—Widnes, one goal one try ; St. Helens Recs. nil. Johnson restarted for Widnes, and kicked into touch. Immediately on the kick out a smart rush was made by the RHO, and the Widnesians had an anxious five minutes. After a good deal of rushing and scrambling. Fyldes got what may be described as a " soft " try for the Rees, the Widnes fowards watching the ball go dead instead of giving it an impetus, the kick at goal was unsuccessful. Taylor made a fine run up, and passed to Brennan. The latter being collared repassed to Taylor,who was grassed on the visitors' goal line. A penalty Wai allowed Wilnes, and Johnsoa essayed at goal. hut.the kick was abortive. Passing be- tween Heleop and Grace saw the former plant the bait down behind the posts, Johnson augumenting. Nothing further was scored on either side, and Widnes won by 1 two goals two tries (ten points), to St. Helens Rees one try (three points).</p>
        <p>"A " TRAM COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>SALFORD V. WARRINGTON—PIayed at Salford on Saturday before 4,000 spectators, Salford started, and Warrington pressed until dribbling by Haillam and Brown took play to the visitors " 25." Hignett made a mark, which further improved Salford's position, and Miles drApped at goal,the ball striking the upright. War rington after this attacked strongly, and had hard lines in not scoring, Roberts holding the hall up. Following the drop out,the home team dribbled down the field, Stocks scoring, but Hignett failed to increase the value. Even play followed, the defence of Jones and Paden be- ing clever,and Salford crossed over, leading by 5 points to nothing. Warrington op-med tto E,!COI2d half strongly, Ashurst kicking a penalty goal, and this advantage was maintained till Miles ram to centre. The visiting f or- svards,however, carried the scrummages and showed the more combination, the backs passing well, and Salford were Ir-pt continually in the defence. Pierson at hat broke away,and was almost scoring,the ball just rolling dead. Warrington again asserted themselves, but could not get through. Final result :—Saltord one goal ens try (five points), Warrington one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V.WIDNES A.—Played on St. Mary's grouni on Saturday. The visitors won the toss. Thomas failing to return the kick, Widnes were soon defending. but soon rushed to half way. From a free kick, the Saints again rushed away, O'Brien saving. From the ensuing scrimmage, Widnes went away, a scrum being formed very near the St. Mary's line. Al- torde was penaliz-el for being offside, the free kick bringing little relief as it was well returned. Wines again pe•ssed, a series of scrimmages being formed near the Saints posts, Roughley relieving and bringing Jones down before returning. Warburton next kicked well down the field. Clarke afterwards made a mark and play was fought in the centre. Corless then tricked his opponents, but from a scrum, the Widnes forwards rushed to the Saints' line,where the juniors' defence was severely tested for sorne time. Corless relieved.:ones re- turning, but Hieginbottom made a mark, ‘Varburton tackling O'Brien before he could return. Roughley was again hard preseed and was thrown in touch near the corner. The Saints kicke away ant one of the visitors making a mark Jones kiclie I a goal. From the kick off, Hughes was C""Piel1,1119 with a good run, lack of filish ,Pelling lb" etL):t. Jones then got away and scored after a good run, the umo player failing to ' augment. Play nested at half-way, whon half-tim was called with Widnes leading by 5 points. 0i re- tearting, Hughes returnee, O'Brien kicking to touch at half way. From the kick out, the Stints rushed off oind play rested on the visitors line, Alford° relieving. The lints were awarded a free kick, Povey getting Alforde b,fore returning. The Saints again pressed, Higgin- bottom getting away nicely but he kicked the ball dead. From the kick out, Roughley was prevented from re- turnine, but the Saints again forced Widnes on tteiir line, without result. Exchange kicking followed,which resulted in Alforhe kickine to touch at halfway. Thc Saints again got away, Johnson having hard lineseosing the ball whilst srounding behind the pests. Not to be denied the St. Mary's men again went away and the Wid- tee defence was severely testel, Higginbettom Mein?. , bard linesalehile directly afterwards,Clarke was collared near the line. Alforde kicked well and from a free kick flieeinbottom failed at goal. Play still rested in the visitors' quarter, breakaways by Widnes being well checked by Corleaa arid Rouehley. Clarke kicked well down the field, Wood ward being too quick fer Jones and preventing a return. ITigginbottom men gained ground by a leng kick and the the SeMary'e pressed but failel to score. Final score :—Widnes A,l goal,l tty,VVarring- ton St. Mary's, nil. - • •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN " v. RUNCORN VICTORIAL.—TheSe local teams met on the Irwelldane enclosure on Saturday, the object being to help the risicg clubsin the district. Tkere wan, however,enly a moderate attendance. A very one sided game ended in a win for the"A.."teans by one goal, seven tries, to nil. RUNCORN Hoatorrs V. RUNCORN PARISR.—Played on the ground of the former. The home captain won the toss, and playing with the wind the team were soon attacking, but the backs were slow to take the chanc,&gt;s which the forwards gave them. The game continued on the Parish line, where Joyce dropped a goal. Play was soon back again, and passing was tried, with the result that from a scrimmage T. Ellis dropped a splendid goal. After the kick-off the home team pressed agair, end for off-side Stringer kicked a penalty goal. Half- time :Horce*:B,six points;Far'sh, nil. The EiJrnets kicked off, and no returti-lieing' Made they began to press. Dean missed the ball, and Smithy taking the chance made a rush for the line. Joyce tackling him just at the limit. Encourageel by this the Parish played up :nstter, but the Hornets started a passing movement, and Stringer ran half the length of the field and kicked. T. Ellis missed a fine noening for a try from a pack. The other wing had a chance which Hearne finished with a good try. Final :—Hornets, nine points ; Parish, nil. WESTON v. LOSTOCR.--At Weston. Lostock won the toss,and played with the wind in their favour. Lightfoot started and play settled in the home half. J.Davies initi- ted a passing movement, bet the tackling of the home team was strong. Past and exciting play was witnessed. J. A. Pencllebury ran half the length of the field,but was brought down before coming dangerous. The home for- wards were now playing a strong game, but could not score. From a scrimmage in the visitors' half Glynn kicked isp.The home backs misjudged the balLandOlynn rushed up and getting the ball, passed to Fish, who scored. J. H. May kicked a grand goal. The whistle blew with Lostock leading by five points to nil. Ward restarted, and the game was of a fast character, both sides trin, visited in turn. Helped by the wind, the home team became dangerous, but the tackling of the visitore was too good for them. A penalty was ordered for offside play. Lightfoot kicked a goal, and from this pint the home team played smarter, but selfishness spoiled grand chances. The visitors went with a rush, Fish charged down Riley's kick, and following up scered,but rhr referee brought the hall hack for a knock on. The final WAS now reached with Weston being beaten for the first time on their own ground by one goal one try (five points) to one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>PARK JUNIORS V. BANK QUAY RANRERS.—This match was playad at Arpiey on Sat ur.lay, and resulted as fol lows :—Park Juniors, one goal one try (five points) ; Bank Qoay Rangers, one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN 4. HAYDOaIC.</p>
        <p>WIGAN COP.</p>
        <p>Therm old rivals met on the Earleintown grou d on Saturday in the second round of the above cup competi- tion before about 1,500 spectators. flaydock kicked off 15 minutes late with a strong wind and the hill in their favour. The homesters early invaded the visitors' goal, but the backs defended well. Raynor being specially no- ticeable for his huge kicking. Although they had a strong wind backing them up,the visitors could make no headway and one or tw-i intrusions into th 3 home goa were neatly foiled by the backs. At this part of thei</p>
        <p>I game there was nothing to choose in the teams, both ends being visited in turn, but both goals remained in- tact. Up to half time no score was made by either side. On resuming the homesters at once assumed the aggressive and a perfect fusilade was made on the visitors' goal, but try as they would, the hotnesters could not get the ball past Lowe. lip to this point flaydock bad rarely crossed the half-way line in the second half, but once they broke away and one of the visitors shot from long range. Chew ran out to meet the ball and in sotna unaccountable fashion only feebly kicked it, the leather ultimately re- bounding.off an Elaydock player into the net. Towards the clog the homesters were a warded a penalty. Platt took the kick but shot right into the goal-keeper's hands. No farther scoring was none and fleydock thus ran out -.inners by the narrow margin of one goal to none.</p>
      </div>
