      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>.tttero to tbe Obitor.</head>
        <p>.tttero to tbe Obitor.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON F.C. AND ST. MARY'S F.C. reference to a letter signed " Warrington St. Mary's R.F.C." which appeared last Saturday. here- with is cory of a letter received from the St. Mary's F.C. in reply to the offer made them. This is the only ccminunication I have had from themand I should like those interested to compare the resolution as given in their letter to me with the one sent to the Press.—Yonrs Sce.,</p>
        <p>J. E. WARREN,</p>
        <p>.• , Hon. Secretary. W.F.C. 14, Arpley-street, War; '^oton, January 16th.</p>
        <p>[cork] Warrington St. 'Mary's F.C., 135, Sankey-street, Warrington. December 16th, 1897.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—A meeting. of our committee Was held last Tuesday, when the following resolution was unanim- ously passed :—"That this comnattree will not entertain the proposal of the Warrington F.C., viz., co sign a paper binding us to assist the club, and to let them take our players at their own discretion,without approaching us first, and obtaining a transfer." If these are yeur only terms. which we think are cut of all reason,we had better let the matter drop.—T. H. ASHCROFT, hon. secretary. Mr. Warren, Arpley-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>MISSION AT GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l wish to state that the mission to be held at the Baptist Church,Golborne-street,as announced in another part of your paper, will be entirely undenominational ; indeed we do not even know to what &amp;nomination the two evangelists belong. They come from the Pente- costal League, which is an inter-denominational organi- sation, founded in 1891, by Mr. Reader flarris,Q.C., and which has already been widely used by God for the deepening cf spiritual life in believers and the salvation of sinners. Mr. Herrin writes " Our methods are the simplest in the world, we lift up, at every meeting, a Saviour who saves to the uttermost. We do not attempt to amuse,entertain, or even educate peopTe; we propose, by the grace of God, to save them, and then send them out to save others. We dare to believe the whole Word of God. We see no reason why the Acts of the Apcstles should not be repeated; we have the same God,the same Saviour,the same Holy Ghost,the same promises and the same commands. We look to the same power; and ex- pect similar results." The afternoon meetings during the week will be mere especially for Christians, and we most heartily ineite Christians of all denominations to Join us in the stte'y of God's Vl ord and in prayer,so that the evening MPf ' • igs may be crowned with the salva- tion of sinners.—Yours truly, JAMES T. SCHOFIELD (Pastor.) 28, Crosfield-Oreet, Warrington. January t7th, 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SPIRITUALISM AND CHRISTI ANITY.</head>
        <p>SPIRITUALISM AND CHRISTI ANITY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l, in common with many others, think it very unwise of the Christians to have provoked the debate on above subject and its sequel of Sunday last" S-cularism and Christianity." Far better to " Let sleeping does lie." Awkward facts are always liable to crop up ou such occasions. They have done so in this case. Some of the statements made have been amazing. At tbe Sunday meeting Mr. F. R. Roberts spoke of the amount of " swallow " required to get down some of the Secula- rist statements. Mr. Roberts it is thought need have no difficulty on this score. The man who can swallow the camel of the Biblical narrative need not strain much at the scientifically attested facts of Secularism. As to Mr. Waldron I was surprised to find with how many of his views I was in full accord in his advocacy of the teaching of Jesus. It was in his attempt to bolster up a man-made theological Christianity where our dissent began. Let the distinc- tion between the two be clearly understood. That which man has made, man can destroy ; the things that are of God are eternal. Theological Christianity is fast crumb- ling to pieces beneath the aseaults of man's investiga- tion and criticism. Hence we see how foolish it is of these good folks to "kick °pies!: the pricks " of facts. And some of the arguments USNi on Sunday last were singularly weak. I have not time, nor you, Sir,—the space to go through them all, but here are two as sam- ples. As an illustration of faith, Mr. Waldron said that if he bought a ticket at a railway station,for Edinburgh. be had faith as to being taken there by the company' Now, Edinburgh is net "the bourne from whence no traveller returns !" Such faith " reste epon experience, either pommel, or that of other credible witnesses ; while the thing its,lf is so easy of proof. Again, speaking of "mysteries" of Christianity and the difficulty some people had in accepting them, he an- nounced the startling fact that you had only to"believe" in order to be able to accept them. Now this fact would require all Mr.Roberts's powers of degluition to success- fully swallow.lt savours a the logicians petitio principii. It is on a par with taking shares in an unknown com- pany in order to ascertain its genuineness. Truly I think Mr. Waldron himself would be loth to invest so -wildly. These are indeed very rotten props to hold up so shakey a fabric. Ido think it a pity men are solo- tent on upholding the perishing fahle-founded creeds of the past as to neglect the sweetness and light, the sim- plicity and common application of the teaching of Jesus. —Truly yours, Penketh, January 24th, 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>JOHN PEERS.</head>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>SABBATH OBSERVANCE. Sir.—With our letter which you kindly published on the 15th inst we had intended to close our share in the this correspondence, hut we feel constrained to trouble you once more. In that letter we said, "Now as the only authority who could rescind that(the Fourth Command. meet) or any other part of the moral law is God, we ask him (R.G.L.) when and where did God rescind the 5. b - eervance of the Fourth Commandment ? Here are the Scriptures which he offers us in reply. " What things soever the law saith, it says to them who are under the law." "Having abolished in his death the enmits,even the law of Commandments contained in ordinances "For I bear them record that they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge. For they being ignor- ont of God's righteousness, and going about to estahlish their own righteousness, have not submitted themselves to the righteousness of God. For Christ is the end nt the law for righteousness to everyone that believeth." And then he adds,as a corollary,"The Eoglish Law is binding on those who are under it, the Mosaio was obligatory en the Jewish nation,and so also the Law of Christ is bind- ing on those who are under that law hence I admit that nine of the Commandments are obligatory on those who are under the law of Christ." And all this le put before us as evidence that one or the most important parts of the moral law was expunged from Goo's Statute Book mere than eighteen hundred years ago. Well, we must at once confess our inability to grasp the bearing of the passages cited and the inference deduced from them on this controversy.We would even qdestion their relevancy to the subject at all. But we leave your readers to draw their own conclusions, retaining our own strong and decided conviction that the fourth commandment is still In operation for the glory of God and the good of his creatures. Nor are we alone in this conviction. The same impression seems to pervade the minds of Christian people all over the world. We have never yet heard of any community of believers in Christ that repudiates the Sabbath Law. There are, unfortunately, many differences amongst Christians as to doctrine and dis- cipline; but so far as we know there is no .diverhence re- garding the Sabbath. Anglicans, Romannits, and NIT). conformists are all mere or less agreed in treating it as a Divine ordinance still binding upon men. Ind hence it is that there is a general suspension of work every seventh day and meetings for the public worship of God. Nor can we omit to say here that to her honour the Church of England is the one which, in this country at least,gives most prominence to the whole moral law. In all her places of worship, from the Caftedral down to the humblest mission church, the Ten C*mmandmeNts,</p>
        <p>printed in large letters, occupy a conspicuous place in the chancel : her ministers read them publicly in the ears of the congregation every Sabbath day ; and the people respond to every sue of them with a prayer.' Lord have mercy upon us, and incline our hearts to keep this law." .I\i"i;w what a farce, nay what a positive mockery of God w-uld all this be in the case of the fourth com- mandment if that part of the moral law is no longer a part of the Law of God. Bat we are tru:y thankful to believe, in common with Christians of every land and tongue, that the fourth commandment is still in force, for never in any former pniod of human history was it more needed than it is in these days. Never were men more prone to forget the heavens and the earth than they are now. Alammom seems to be the god of the world at present,and his wor- ship entails so much grinding and oppression of the poor that our ears are continually filled with cries frem the mines and ot'ser centres of labour for a lessening of the ' tale of bricks and for a "living wage." Is this a time, then, for throwing aside the gracious enactment I whose design is to bring men nearer to their Creator, and to bless them with a whole day's rest from toil and care every week. Ought we not rather do all in our power to guard it against the encroachments of the world and, as far as possible, endeavour to spread its purifying and elevating influence among our fellow men.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
