      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>HON OURS EVEN.</p>
        <p>There was more than the ordinary quantity of interest taken in the league match between War- rington and Salford at Wilderspool on Saturday. To find the reason for this we need not look far. The Reds have made great strides since last season, and by dint of much enterprise in importation have got together a team which can give points in clever- ness to most of its rivals. There is a big Welsh element in the combination,but that is only a "sign of the times" and shows Salford's up-to-dateness. Farly in the season Warrington succumbed by a good majority at New Barns and Salford therefore were heroes to a slight extent. The crowd numbered about 7,000, and the best possible football weather prevailed. A strong breeze made matters rather awkward one way. Boscow cried off the Warring- ton team, ITeesom filling the vacancy, and Cross re- turning to his place at centre-threequarter. Salford appeared as selected, Thomas being an enforced spectator through his trawler difficulties. Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes) was referee,and had the follow- ing players in his charge : Warrington: Heesom, full back; Moores, Cross, Hallam, and Isherwood, threequarters ; Bate and Duckworth, half-backs; Deakin, Taylor, Roby Mason, Barrett, Hill, Swift, and Jolley, forwards. Salford : Smith, full back ; Hoskins, T. Williams (cap.) Harter, and Pearson, threequarters ; Grey and Griffiths, half-backs; J. Williams, Rhapps, Tunney, Moss, Davies, W. Brown, Fisher, Llewellyn, forwards. The spin of the coin gave Warrington the choice of positions, and the home team immediately took advantage of the wind and sun. Moss kicked off and the ball travelled to Moores who returned strongly to the Salford 2s, but the home for wards were wrongly called back from bore by the refe- ree,play resting in Warrington territory.Griffithe started a promising passing bout,Harter spoiling the effort,and a similar movement by Gray was nipped in the bud by Bate. A couple of clever kicks by Hallam caused an in- cursion into Salford territory. Here the ball travelled in touch and Heesom centreing well, Smith was compelled to kick dead. The Salfordians had an anxious time, the Warrington onslaughts being difficult to deal with. A clever cross kick from Bate beat Smith and a score would have followed but for the lucky intervention of Griffiths. A series of scrimmages was fought out on the line, Duckworth being held just inside the rut. Griffiths raised the siette with a lying kick which Duckworth re- turned and" Smack " collared the dropping ball at full speed only to run straight into the arms of the Salford captain, T. Williams. Bate next get penalised and this gave the Reds a much needed breather. They could not clear nut the aggressive Warringtonians how- ever though they defended determinedly. Pear- son was prominent with a smart run and tackle of Heesom, and this led to a change of front, Salford obtaining the upper band. A smart sequence of exchanges between Gray and Hos- kins resulted in the former making a dash for the line, Heesom dropping him in close quarters. A penalty against Salford was taken by Hallam, hut the ball tra- velled strongly beck from Tunney. Gray again rounded the scrimmage in fine style, but his pass went forward and the home line escaped. A judicious punt by Griffiths transferred the game to the flame goal,and securing the ball again,another brilliant passing movement followed. Hoskins missing an easy chance in the corner. Site and Isherwood ran the ball out to centre in clever fashion. Cress mulled terribly, and Heesom luckily punted the ball clear, Pearson coming in for a rough tackle by Moores and Mason. From a scrimmage at centre Bate threw the ball to I-herwood, but the watchful Williams intercepted it,and was going strongly for the line when the whistle sounded. This was an escape of the narrowest character for Warrington. Isherwood punted and ran cleverly, Smith being forced to kick out f the enclosure to save his charge. A dodgy iun by Barter slightly relieved. Williams and Hoskins on the right wing improved mat- ters but were soon pulled up. Moores next troubled Smith who fell on the ball in the nick of time. Sharp tackling by the Warrington backs kept the Reds busy defending. Mason rushed up with the ball and after a fine run was bowled over by Smith. At last the score came. Taylor scooped up the oval, and giving Deakin S nice pass, the latter ran in near the goal. Cross failed to augment. Williams snapped up the ball and made a splendid run to the home half, Pearson missing his final pass however, and Moores calmly returned. Bate nearly get in, but his pass was forward. Then Dakin threw the ball away whea on the line, and just before the in- terval Cross tried a drop at goal, which was a long way out. Half time, Warrington 1 try, Salford nil. On restarting, Sate put five three-quarters in the field with a view to checking the Salford back play. War- rington early assumed the aggressive, Barrett running nearly to the line before throwing to his captain who was deliberately t•houldered off. Deakin followed Emit and Smith had ti, kick in touch at the corner. Cross again. let his side dervn with poor fielding, end the Sal- ford front rat. rushed to the other end. J. Williams had a elnrinus chance hut threw the ball wildly. A fine run by Hoskins 'MI Williams ended in the touch-line being crossed. A morns at later the home forwards were coolly put off side by T. Williams, hut for a time War- rington held the upper hand. Smith had mere work clean he could manage,Hoskine giving him every assistance. A spell of loose play led to Salford tahlng nip a position near the line. Duckworth attempted to kick out, Griffiths charged the ball down and placed the teem, on equal terms. The same placer's goal kick from A diffienit angle failed. Harter, Williams,and Hoskins's put in some combined play, the latter being just stopped • in the corner. The Reds bad chances of scoring innumerabie, but through forward passing threw many an eneiling away. A cross kick by cores put We fOriV,AftiN in pos. secsion and a long dribble was not checked until the line was almost reached. Isherwood and Bate made several clever movements but the Salford datence was sound. Duckworth and Moores looked like breaking through but Grey intervened and snapped up the ball at a critical moment. Hoskins got clear away and ran half the length of the field before being hemmed in when he threw the ball wildly. Moores responded With a similar dashing run on the other wing and when he had only the full baola to deal with he lost the bill. Thia was the last chance of scoring and the game ended in a draw of a try each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, J ANHARY 28, 1898</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, J ANHARY 28, 1898</p>
        <p>FIVE RUNCORNIANS FOR SOUTH</p>
        <p>AFRICA.</p>
        <p>There left Runcorn t.-day' (Friday), for South Africa, three well-known Runcorn gentlemen,namelp,Mr.Thoin as Lea, confectioner, Cireenway-ro Ad; Mr. T. Reynolds, and Mr. J. A. Smith, of Greenway-road. Mr.L.:4a is a na,m- ber of the Runcorn Guardians, and all-) an energetic member of the Liberal Club. His health for the past three years has been anything but good, and with the hope of improvement he has undertaken this journey. Mr. H. Reynolds is a trustee of the Primitive Methodist Church,Greenway-road, and is taking ths trip for no special purpose, beyond an outing. Mr. Smith was the secretary of the Pickwick Literary Society, and on Tues- day a pleasant evening was spent, as a leave-taking. Mr. Smith was also connected with St. Paul's Wesleyan Chapel. Mr. Smith has procured a situation in the Trans- vaal under his father who has been out there some time. Mr. Lea and Mr. Reynolds left Runcorn this morning. by the eleven o'clock train to London, where they will be joined by Mr. Smith later in the day. Then they will proceed to Southampton whore they will go on board the " Scott" one of the Union Liners. Mr. Lea and Mr. Reynolds are not going for any stated length of time. Two other gentlemen, viz., Mr. H. Dutton son of Mr. Dutton of Wilson's Hotel, and Mr. W. Brown, are also intending to make their way to Cape Town. Thole gentlemen should have departed in a week or two, but a suitable boat could not be secured, and they are to leave English shores by the " Gothic," a White Star liner at the beginning of March. These gentlemen have already secured situations in Cape Town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FRODSHA.3I.</head>
        <p>FRODSHA.3I.</p>
        <p>Fie CHURCH COUNCIL—On Monday evening the annual gathering of the above Council was held in the Trinity Wesleyan Church, Frodsham. In the afternoon a sermon was preached by the Rev. Charles H. Kelly, president of the London Free Church Council. In the evening a public meeting was held in the same place or worship when speeches were delivered by several local ministers and gentlemen. A BIRTHDAY PARTY—On Monday ovening,Mr.William Youd,landlord of the Bear's Paw,Frodsham,gave a party to his friends on the occasion of his birthday. Over 90 invitations had been sent out,and about 70 persons put in an apgearance. A splendid dinner,consisting of roast beef, duck, geese, Zso., was laid on the tables,and the guests quickly availed themselves of the opportunity. The following toasts were drunk—" The Queen and Royal Family," "Army and Navy," "Health of Mr. and Mre.Youd and family, "The Town and Trade of Fred- sham," and " Farmers and Agriculture." After dinner the party indulged in games, So., Mr. Thomas Baxter, C.C., of -Dutton Old Hall, presiding over this part of the proceedings. He spoke of Mr. Youd's long connection with Frodsharn and the Bear's Paw. He (the Chairman) knew the two previous landlords of that house,viz., Mr. Warburton and Mr. King. The latter held the license 21 years, and the present proprietor had held it 22 years. (Applause.) Songs were then rendered by Messrs. John Hatton (Lymm), R. Rogers, A. Jones, T.Holland, J.Cot- terill, and J. Leach. Mr. Hibbert accompanied on the piano. Amongst those present were—Messrs. Horton (Bell-hare Farm) ; J. E. Bate (Sutton) ; R. Bate (Nether- ton) ;R. Wade (Acton Grange) ; D.Oultram (Kingsley); S. Lewis (Kingsley) ; A. Gibson, H. Davies, T. Edwards, A. Hutchinson, and Ridden.</p>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. WILLIAM CLARKE.—The death of Mr. William Clarke. aged about 50 years, grocer, Church-et., Frodsham, took place on Sunday merning after an illness which extended over a period of four weeks. His health had been failing for some time, but on New Year's day he received a stroke and I was obliged to take to his bed. Dr. Selby was his medi- cal attendant and all that medical aid could do for him was done by that gentleman. The deceased gentleman, although he had not held any public office in connection with the affairs of the village, was respected and esteemed by all classes, and when it became known that he had passed away a gloom was cast over the village. Mr. Clarke leaves a widow (hut no family). The - mains were interred on Wednesday afternoon in the burial place attached to the Parish Church. The funeral was largely attended and many of the residents showNi their respect to the decnsed by drawing their blinds whilst the mournful cortege passed up the road. A TRIP TO JERUSALEM.—On Wednesday evening in the Town Hall Frocleham, the RTC Samuel Schov, a native of Jerusalem, delivered a lecture on a trip to his native city. The lecture proved very interesting and at the close a eollecti in was made on behalf of the London Society for promoting Christianity among the Jews. .</p>
        <p>- TRINITY MUTUAL SOCIETY.-05 Wednesday OVerliflgat the meeting of the above society, a paper was read on the subj-ct "The Llrd's Suppsr versus the Mass." Grat int.remt wag taken in the paper by the audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN riDEPENDENT LABOUR PARTY.</head>
        <p>THE EA.RLESTOWN riDEPENDENT LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. KIER HARDIE.</p>
        <p>On Friday. a meeting under the above auspices was held in the Town Hall, Eerlestown, but the attendance over which the Rev. H. Bedell Smith presideo. was net a large one. Mr. Kier Flardie'a subject was " Lessons of the Great Lockout." The Chairman defirel Socialism in a somewhat lengthy fashion, poimine out that while it was a scien- tific it was also a humane movement. It was designed to succour the weak and defenceless and uplift the downtrodden as against the theory of the weakest going to the wall. It Was worthy the support of all righteous and thoughtful men and eras to benefit the whole of mankind. Mr. Kier Flardie,who met with a good reception,then addressed loimPelf to Ilia s.itject. After some prefatoly remarks as to how the gceat lockout had been brought about and the use of Rome arguments in favour of the eight hours' working day he spoke of the lessons to be learned from the lockout. First,he said, it seemed to portend a clear d rarring together of the various trade unions of the country. That was primarily an engineers' fight, but behind the engineering employers stood the employing classes of the country. In like manner, be- hind the ,ngineers ought to have stood the trade union- ists of the country.—(epplauSe.)espective of their calling, and if the dispute had for its outcome the crea- tion of a hue. trade -union for the nation it would not have been fought in vain. Just as it was important for every workman to belong to his trade union, so was it equally important that each trade union should belong to one another. (Applause.) And they must not wain ill acme great labour trouble again faced them bernre deg. ing with the matter. The ti me for trades federation was here and now. Suppose the workers had their federation complere, they might rely up, nit that the employers would have their federation equally complete. The re- suit would be two hostile forces facing each other con- tinuously and each seeking to obtain an adoantage over the other. Perscnally, despite the apparent contradic- tion of his record in politics, he Was a man of peace. He hated war and all that savoured of war and held it to be an insult to the intelligence of the democracy of Europe that peace was only maintained to-day by turning each European nation into a huge armed power. The peace which was based upon the bayonet was not the pace which any Cane man would desire, and in like manner those who looked forward to the future and imagined peace be- tween capital and labour because each ride was fully armed founded their hopes upon a delusion. Oet- side the question of trade organisation there was a third party—the State—and whoever controlled the third party must ultimately become master of the situation. Whenever force was required to back ur. the Capitalistic system that force was always forthcoming whether Llherels or Tories occupied the majority of seats in the House of Commons, So long as the present state cat things continued wealth W4B bound to rule and if work- men' had coMplete and perfect federation there would still remain a tlnies of the nation. The reply would be as hi was qt.eation of who was to centre! the des- that those who owned the ration were its rightful con- trollers. One of the lessons tbe lockout ought to drive home to the people Was that they must change the ownership of land and property. That was what was meant by Socialism. He ridiculed a few wrong im- pressions circulated as to what Sacialism was, and then proe.eededi to speak on the economic side of the question. 'Fonching noon the two chief parties in the House of Commons Mr. Kier Hardie said that those who were Liberals and Tories during the last election were not Liberals and Tories during the lockout but were only employers on the one hand and locked out workmen on the other. While the workman learned to vote as he had learned to strike the day for strikes would be over and gone.</p>
        <p>TEA. PARTY AT DARESBLTRY. The Christmas tea party for the scholars attend- ing the C,lurch Sunday School took place in the Board Bchool,llaresbury, on Monday. An excellent tea was partaken of by about 70 scholars, after which amusements of diff-irent kinds occupied the time until prizes for regular attendance and good conduct throughout the year were distributed by the Vicar. After receiving the prizes, three cheers were gives for the teachers and for those who had assisted in the preparation ot tea. Amusements again occupied the time until about 9, when each child was the recipient of an orange and cake, cheers being given for the Vicar and Mrs. Clark as the children dispersed.</p>
        <p>MAZAWATTNIII MAZANATTHB DELICIOUS MAZA.WATISH FRAGRANT. MAZAWATTER REFRESHING. I‘l AZ kwAprHii ECONoMICAL. MAZAWATICHR Bold by HENRY MILLING and Co., LIMITED, f, Hursemarket-strent, and Corporation Buildings. Sole WhOlesale and Retail Agents for Warringtoa. • LINSEED CoMPat-ND (Trade Mark) for Cougtus &amp;nil olds. &amp;L, 134. Sold by Chemists only.--4141.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN. j THE PEA.RSON AND KNOWLES COAL AND IRON COMPANY, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN. j THE PEA.RSON AND KNOWLES COAL AND IRON COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.)</p>
        <p>The Market ground on Friday night presented quite a lively appearance. In addition to the or- ainary gathering ef stalls and purchasers, the Church Association occupied a small corner around which a large number ,it people gathered. It was feared that those who did not approve of the teach- ings of the Protestant League would make some disturbance. There was certainly some slight hoot- ing and interruption, but any organised attempt at violence was foiled by the police, who were assem- bled in strong force. To an unbiassed mind the necessity for any interruption or disturbance was difficult to find,but the opinions set forth were, as was to be expected, not in accordance with the views of the wholb of the people assembled.</p>
        <p>The late cycle parade has at last reached the final stage,and on February 10th the old people,for whose benefit the parade was held, will have an opportu- nity of enjoying a social evening in the Co-opera- tive Hall,and of talking over in their own way,mat- ters which are now considered ancient history. As stated elsewhere all applications for tickets must reach the committee on or before Saturday the sth February, in order that proper arrangements may be made for entertaining the old folks,</p>
        <p>The question of enlarging the Town Hall should I think be a matter which in the near future will have to be seriously considered. On several occa- sions it has been found to be considerably inade- quate to accommodate the clamber of people who have required to gain admission. When it was first erected it was pointed out frequently that it was too small, and subsequent events have proved that the statement was perfectly correct. It was cer- tainly a serious blunder to construct the building as if it would serve for all time, without allowing for extension. Now it is seen that the place should really be enlarged, but doubtless the expense of such an undertaking:will be a stumbling *olock. However, it is necessary, and the Council might do worse than give the subject a share of their attention.</p>
        <p>CLOSING OF THE INCE WORKS. Notices were posted at the Inca forges, belonging to the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company Limited, to the effect that operations will cease on Tuesday sth of February. The explanation of the notice is that, owing to foreign competition and a desire on the part of the directors to concentrate their establishment, it has been decided to close the Inca works and to confine their iron manufa.:ture to the Hallam Forge at Warrington. A large num- ber of men are affected, but it is understood that the company will endeavour to find employment for as many men as possible at Warrington. The following is a copy of the notice issued by the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Limited, Warrington, in regard to their closing of forges at Ice, near Wigan, alluded to in the para- graph above : Notice is hereby gives, that these works will be closed on Saturday, Fobru Iry sth, and the engagement of all the men now employed at the company's Wigan forges will terminate on that date. It is with 'reluctance and very great regret that the directors have come to this decision, as they f, fly appreciate the loyalty with which the men have in the face of considerable opposi- tion seconded their efforts to cheapen production by the adoption °Utile three-shift system of working. This ex- periment has to some extent succeeded, but the steady growth of foreiin competition and the constantly in- creasing use of steel in substitution for wrought iron makes it necessary, at any rate for the present, to con- centrate all the company's puddling plant at Warring- ton,so as to secure the most econorniaal results. Work will be found for as many old hands as possible,and it is hoped for all who wish it, at the Bewsey or Dallarn Forges, and applications for situations should be made forthwith to the works manager, Mr. C. Bunch, at War- rington. THE PEARSON AND KNOWLES COAL AND IRON COMPANY, LIMITED, WARRINGTON. January 21, 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>MUSICAL FESTIVAL- AT ,F,ARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL FESTIVAL- AT ,F,ARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a large audience gathered in St* John's Church, Earlestown, for the purpese of participa- ting in a musical festival arranged by Mr. H. W. Rad- ford, the talented organist, for the purpose of raising funds for the renovation of the organ. A formidable chorus had 'eeen got together, singers being drawn from the choirs of the various places of worship in the town, whilst many singers not attached to any choir, volun- teered their services. In order to ensure success Mr. Radford has put the chorus through a lengthy and thorough training with the result that an evenly bal- anced chorus was produced. A quartette of excellent so- loists was engaged, including Miss Louise Cestria.R.C.M, (soprano); Miss Alice Richardson (contralto), Manchester and Liverpool concerts:Mr. Walter Lawley (tenor) Man- chester; and Mr. George H. Ditchburn (bass) master of chorikters, Manchester Cathedral. The programme was a purely sacred one, and the first part consisted of the Christmas selections from Handel's Messiah. Mr. Lawley sepened with " Comfort ye," and the excellent manner in which he sang this beautiful recit. showed at ouce that Mr. Radford had chosen artists of first class merit, Mr. G. 11. Ditchburn followed with " Thus rsaith the Lord," and he was not a whit behind Mr. Lawley in ex- cellence. Be possesses a powerful bass voice, and uses 'it with excellent judgment. Miss Alice Richardson as a contralto sang with praiseworthy effect, each of her selections being rendered in a most efficient manner. Miss Louise Cestria is not a stranger at Earlestown, and it goes without saying that she acquitted herself re- markably well. The choruses were well sustained, and there did not appear to be a weak spot. The second part of the programme, consisted of a quartette " God is a spirit," and chorus). " Nazareth," Mr. G. H. Ditchburn and and "Hear my Prayer," (Miss Louise Cestria chorus, duett " Love Divine," Miss L. Cestria and Mr. W. Lawley. Mr. Radford presided at the organ and was responsible throughout for the service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PROPOSED MEMORIAL TO THE</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED MEMORIAL TO THE</p>
        <p>COUNTESS OF LA.THOM.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>With the object of raising funds by local subscriptions for a memorial to the late Lady Lathom,which it is sug- kested should take the form of an endowment for the perpetual maintenance of a district nurse, who will be attached to the cottage hospital at Ormskirk, and who will work ecpecially in the districts of Lathom and Skelmersdale, a meeting was held at the Town Hall on Too., 1 y aftp7no-m. The Mayor(Alderman Wright) pre. idl, and ther• were a!so present :—The Rev. Canon eenrhyn. the Rev. Canon Blundpll, the Rev. Morley 9-evsmson, Miss Bessie Dutton, Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, and Mr. W. L. Hutton, of Ormskirk (previsional hon. secretary) The MAYOR expressed his thorough sympathy with the movemont. . .</p>
        <p>Canon BLLTEDELL said the great object of the meeting was to move as many as they could to take an interest in the scheme. There were many in South West Lanca- shire who, he felt sure, would be glad to record their grateful memory of the noble lady whose death they all deplored. They wished to establish a foundation that should keep up her memory in perpetuity—ln fact to found a sum of money to maintain a district nurse. The districts of Lathom and Skelmersdale had been selected because it was in those places that her ladyship was best known and respected. (Applause.) Mr. BATTON said it was not intended to be a purely local scheme,but would embrace South West Lancashire. The trustees of the fund would be the Mayors for the time being of all the corporate towns within that part ofsLancashire. It Was hoped to receive from £l,ooa to £2,000. The memorial suggested would be an appropri- ate and useful one, Miss DUTTON said she had a great admiration for Lady Lathim, and was Jo sympathy with the memorial. She woili b" happy to give a small subscription to the M.rnorial Fund. The Rev. Canon PEERnyit regretted the small attend- ance but he felt sure it did not arise from a want of in- terest in the subject as from the fact that the meeting had not been widely known, Ater referring to the work of the late Lady Latham particularly in South We Lancashire, he moved the following resolution "That this meeting approves of the proposal to raise a memorial to the late Lady Lathom by the foundation of an endowment for the perpetual maintenance of a district nurse, who shall be attached to the Cottage Hospital at Ormakirk, and who shall work esp3cially in the districts of Lathom and Skelrnersdale." ' The Rev. MORLEY STEvairsote seconded the resolution, land said th,it, is common with Canon Penrhyn, he very much deplored the small attendance. The Work of Lady Lathom,as far as it had ti.,ea chiefle known in {Farring- ton, had ben especially amongst women. She had iden- tified herself with the work of the Girla'Friendly Society, and the last occasion she was in Warrington was not more than a year alo, when she addressed his students. He felt that had the meeting been more widely known and that it was one to which ladies would have been welcome there would have been a larger attendance. He was sure there were many ladies who would willisigly respond to the appeal. The motion having been carried. the Rev. Canon BLVWDB" Moved the appointment of a provisional com- mittee, with the addition of the following local gentle- men :—The Mayor of Warrington, the Worshipful Mas- ters of Warrington for the time bring; Mr. Jenkins, Mr. Edward Rise, Mr. William Taylor, and Mr. W. B. Roberts.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. J. JiesitiNs seconded the proposition, and said he was prompted to attend that meeting by the kindly recollections he had of Lady Latham when he was associated with Ormskirk and district, and when his friend Mr. Hutton, whom he was glad to see present, and himself were younger men. After alluding to some of these assQmations, Mr. Jenkins remarked that tie Freemasons of Warrington had liberally subscribed to commemorate the momory of Lady Latborn. The Mayor's services in the chair were acknowledged by the meeting on the propositha of the Rev. Canon PENRWYN who stated that the proposed memorial bad Llrd Ltvhom's hearty sauction.—The Mayor ackuois• hedged the compliment, and expressed the hope that the proposed memorial would be successfully carried through.</p>
        <p>W AUHINGTOH SUNDAY SCHOOL Tx ACKEIte</p>
        <p>AssociATioN.</p>
        <p>The gaarterly meeting of this association was held on Monday evening in Si. Peter's Schoolroom, J.lin-street, when there was a good attendance. After tea the Rev. W. Clark Hudson, of Liverpool, hon. secretary of the London Teachers' gxamina- tion Society, and representing the Liverpool Diocesan Sunday School Institute, delivered a very interesting address on "The Art of Teaching," giv- ing the members some excellent advice. After- wards the prizes and certificates won at the London teachers' examination last April by Warrington Sunday school teachers were presented, Miss Mar- garet White holding tile highest local position in the list A discussion on Mr. Hudson's address fol- lowed, in which the Vicar of St. Peter's (the Rev. B. Percival), who nrilsided, the Rev. W. Hart- wright, the Rev, S. J.Fleming,and Mr. A. J. Jolley, and others took part.</p>
