      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>GERMAN IRON-PRODUCTION.</head>
        <p>GERMAN IRON-PRODUCTION.</p>
        <p>According to the returns just issued by the Associa- tion of German Iron and Steel Mlsters, the total output of pig iron in the German Empire (including Luxem- burg) during the month of December last amounted to the record figure of 613,455 tons, as compared with 599,125 tons in November, 1897, and 552,719 tons in December,lB96. This shows an increase of 16,330 tons, or 272 per cent, on the preceding month, and of 62,736 tons,. or 1135 per cent on the corresponding month of last year. During the calendar year 1897 the tot II pro- duction amounted to 6,889,067 tons, as compared with 6,360,982 tons in 1896, an increass of 528,025 tons, or 8.30 per cent. Since 1891 the increase has made steady progress year by yaar, but the growth has been much more raril during the last three years than ever before, and 1897 shows in round figures a rise in the output of 50 per cent over 1891. The following are the details (in tons) 12 months, De0.,1897. Dee ,1893. 1897. Puddling and Spiegel iron 139.77) 143.300 1619,556 Bessemer 48,153 41,612 577,917 Thomas 319,420 283,395 3,565,156 Foundry Total Total in November. 599,125 544,667</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CHILD MURDERS IN KENT.</head>
        <p>CHILD MURDERS IN KENT.</p>
        <p>SEVENTEEN INFANT-4 STRANGLED. Some startling disclosures were made at an inquest held at Cheriton, Kent. on Wednesday, respecting the death of an infant child,whose body was found in a well at Shorncliffe Camp. The Coroner for East Kent (Mr. R. M. Mercer), in opening the inquiry, stated that this was the fifteenth child upon which he had held inquest under suspicious circumltances within a short period. He thought it was quite time that some turther stops were taken to disaover the murderers of those infants, and he accordingly adjourned the inqust to give the police time to unravel the mystery. The body of a newly-born child was also found concealed in a box at Ashford on Wednesday. The corpse when discovered was still warm, and around the neck and mouth was tightly bound a quantity of linen. A post-mortem exam- ination showed that death was duo to asphyxia, pro- duced by strangulation. Inquiries naade show that there is no doubt that the whole of the 17 infants whose bodies have been fottad in the district have been deli- berately murdered, for in each case death was due to strangulation.</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A JUBILEE INCIDENT.-</p>
        <p>BRAVERY REWARDED. An interesting gathering was held in the Druids Hall, Warrington, on Tuesday evening, when mem- bers of the local friendly societies attended to pre- sent to Mr. H. Rowe, of the Prince of Wales Lodge of Oddfellowg (Manchester Unity) a substantial re- cognition of his gallantry on July 24. On that date the Friendly Societies Jubilee Celebration was held and while the procession was passing through the streets,a horse took fright and dashed off towards the people. Harry Rowe,as he is better known, at the risk of his life,seiz id the animal by the neck and stopped its mad career. He was injured in the effort and had to be taken to the Infirmary. The testimonial presented to h4.m coosi4ted of a purse of gold £23 53. and a jewel badge of merit suitably inscribed.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk (Mr. J. L. Whittle) presided and:after detailing the incident which had led to the gathering he said that Mr. Rowe succeeded in stop- ping the horse when it and the vehicle were only a few feet away from the children, and but for his daring and noble act he had no doubt that many lives would have been lost. Mr. Rowe had the satisfaction of knowing that he did his duty nobly and his gallant conduct saved the lives of some of his fellow creatures. The children who took part in the procession had every reason to feel grateful whenever they heard the name of " Harry Rowe." It made one proud to feel that we had men among us in Warrington of the stamp, mettle, and bravery of Mr. Rowe. He had reason to believe that Mr. Rowe's brave act has not been overlooked in higher quarters, and that before long a further public re- cognition of what he had done would be made. (Apnlause.) Eloquent eulogies of Mr. Rowe were delivered by Bros.J. &amp;aeon ((Milletlows), chairman of the exe- cutive; O.Maybury (Voresters),J.Hall, (Rechabites,) Rev. Father Stanley and others. The presentation was made by Mr. D. Houghton, who eloquently and graphically described the scene of July 24th,afei the circumstances of Rowe's brave act. He said it was to assure Mr. Rowe that such deeds were not forgotten—could not be forgotten for they were written on their memories for all time—that they were met together that night to offer him some proof of their admiration of his gal- lantry. (Applause.) Many of the Orders were not aware of the deed for many hours afterwards, and were not present to join in the hearty Bravo! which_ was thankfully exclaimed. Several months had passed and they were assembled in their representa- tive character to right heartily shout Bravo! laarry Rowe! and present him with their offering of a purse of gold, and silver merit Jewel, as an expression of their admiration of him, and best wishes for his future. (flear, hear and applause.) Concentrated in this form was a complete acknowledgment of all who took a part in the demonstration, and of the true brotherly feeling which happily characterised the affiliated bodies of the district. This heroic act had been spoken of by others in authority, and the courage Harry Rowe had displayed just at the right moment, if the accident was to be avoided, entitled him to a very high place in the esteem and regard of his Society and Order. (Ap- plause.) It was fitting that the presentation should take place in that Hall because the work of organis- ing the procession was carried on there, and now they saw it finally completed. They hoped that every coin Mr. Rowe took from the purse, either tor spend or invest for the welfare of his family, would be productive of as much happiness as it had given those of his brethern and townsmen, who so cheer- fully and heartily b q..tged him receive it. In con- clusion, Mr. Houghton wished Mr. RbWe along and prosperous life. (Applause). Mr. Rowe assured the meeting of his heartfelt gratitude at their recognition of his act. He hoped they would not expect to moke another speech —he would rather stop another horse than do so— (laughter and applause), bet he sincerely thanked the committee and members for their handsome presentation. (Applause). —At the suggestion of the Chairman, it was decided that a copy of Bro. Ifoughton's speech (of which we have only been able to give a summary), should he handed to Bro. 'lowa as a moment° of the occasion. During the evening songs were rendered by Messrs. Emson, Littlewood, and Dnnbobbin.</p>
        <p>ORFORD BARRACKS SY:RC/EA—NTS V. WARRINOTON POLICE. —Played on the Barracks ground on Thursday afternoon. The police kicked off and the Sergeants dribbling down the field confined the play for the first few minutes in the Police quarters. The police now had a turn of press- ing, and Hughes and Newey narrowly missed scoring. From a melee in front of the Police goal Graham scored. Even play followed until Nacres+ secured, but he missed scoring when at short range. Half-time—Sergeants 1 goal, Police nil. In the second half the Sergeants added 5 more goals, the scorers being Rice 2, Eaves 1, Sheridan 1. Newsy scored twice for the Police. Fitts' : Sergeants 5 goals, Police 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>, SPIRITUALISM. ADDRESS ON SPIRITUALISM.-A BUDGET OF QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>, SPIRITUALISM. ADDRESS ON SPIRITUALISM.-A BUDGET OF QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>" Why I am a Spiritualist " was the subject of an ad- dress by Mr. Wallis, tbe editor of " The two Worlds," at the Co-operative Hall, Winwictc-street, on Monday evening. Tnere was a large attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. Pemberton, and in the course of a few I introductory remarks he referred to the meeting held in the Parr Hall, the previous evening. He said the chair- man at the meeting admitted his ignorance of what Spiritealism was, and yet he so far forgot himself as to call Spiritualists tricksters and frauds. To say the least of it, such language was very incautious. (Hear, heir.) He (the chairman) was not going to q iarrel with anyone with reference to their religious beliefs. He gave credit to every man and every woman that they nelieved in their faith honestly and from their hearts, and he would not stoop to misjudge one of them. (Applause.) Though he did not go under the name of a Christian, he did stand for that which was right and true. If he found truth, he claimed to have a perfect right to believe in it; he also claimed the right to think for himself, and he hoped that whoever took opposite views to him would not forget themselves and cease to he gentlemen. Call- ing names was not producing facts. (Applause.) Mr. WALLIS then delivered his lecture. He divided thinkers into two classes—Materialists and Spiritualists. He contended that every Christian was a Spiritualist. The Christian believed Gad to be a spirit : man to be a spirit, awe further, he believed in a continual spirit con- sciousness after death and also in the return to the spirit. Spiritualists might be divided into two classes, the an- cient and the modern. The Christian was the ancient Spiritualist; he believed in bygone spirit manifestations as recorded in the Bible, such as healings,visions.transi- tions. and apparitional manifestations. The modern Spiritualist based his claim upon phenomena which he had witnessed himself, arid upon facts which had oc- curred in his experience. Modern Spiritualism was hardly 50 years old, and in that time it had increased and mul- tiplied until it had spread throughout the whole world. Our human nature and needs prompted us to seek to know something of the world to which we were going. Modern Spiritualism was the science or art of the coin- munion of spirits. He as a Spiritualist was able to say that he had had experience and witnessed manifesta- tions and received messages from the world beyond. He had got beyond a belief, it was a fact of knowleege which he had had communicated from the world which , the people called dead. He wanted them to bear in mind I that it could only be settled by experiment , and investigation ; it could not be settled by debate. Spiritualists challenged investigation. In their literature they gave advice to inquirers, and rules were given by which they might develop their own spiritual gifts. There were abundant facts from scientific men who had given their testimony in favour of Spiritualism. The speaker proceeded to deal with the objections raised by Mr. Waldron, and afterwards dealt with the philosophy of Spiritualism, stating that the body was merely the instrument through which the man obtained his experience. Ho was being trained, i educated and disciplined for a larger life to come. Man was a spirit temporarily associated with the physical body that he might become censcious of his own nature and needs. There was some hissing by the audience when the speaker said that JMIS was a heretic to the religion of his age. The speaker retorted that the same spirit that crueified Jesus was present in the audience. Because Jesus was not orthodox he was crucified. A numb, rof questions were asked and answered at the close, scone of which were as follows :—Why should Christianity and Spiritualism be antagonistic ? I have no objection to being friends with the Christians if they will let us alone.—lf there was no Chrisecan you prove there is a God ?—The question with reference to the existence of God has been discussed by many peopl f. I cannot and would not attempt to prove the existence of God. I have more reverence in my constitution. My idea is that the infinite Spirit is so far beyond our com- prehension that the only thing we can do is to try and follow after the truth in regard to the infinite spirit. As man is intelligent and religious, and moral and spiritual, then there must be a universal spirit. Because I am, God is. I believe I feel there is a supreme infinite wisdom,and there I air prepared to leave it. With reference to Christ, the name means annointed, and he was supposed to be an individual who was anneinted of God. Some be- lieve he was the" Messiah," the Jews don't. lam not prepared to settle the question.—How was it Mr. Bib- binge could not answer one single question put by Mr. Waldron ?—Mr. Bibbings undoubtedly did his best, and he certainly answered some questions. I have had a week to think over the questions raised ; Mr. Bibbings had to speak on the spur of the moment.—Do departed spirits know our movements and our conduct ? If we wake up "en the other Fide " a spiritual being we shall still be spiritual beings with power to think, power to see, and understand. I think It would be one of the most natural things that you should come back and communicate with the loved ones you had left here. Why was it unreasouahle to think that spirit people out of the body should not come' and help Us on earth ? The Bible tells us there are a cloud of witnesses sent forth to witness. These ministering spirits are all round about UP.—Ths speaker at the Spiri- tualist meeting yesterday contended that Spiritualists had done greater work than Christ had done. Rave they raised anybody from the dead or given the blind man sight ?—I don't believe in impossibilities any more than Mr. Waldron does. When the body is dead and the spirit is separated from it I don't believe it could ever be brought back to life. Spiritualists den% claim to do anything like the miracles that are recorded of Jesus Christ. We do claim that there are certain phenomena which are perfectly natural, and which prove the existence of intelligent beings in the world called dead.—Do you believe in the Trinity as taught in the New Testament • if not, why net ?—it is an open question whether the New Testament dnes teach the Trinity. Many people say no. The only passage which Wight it has been altered in the Revised version. The Trinity of father,mether, and chi'd antedated Christian- ity altogether. It is found in the religion of ancient Egypt,aed it was also a universal idea in India. Christia- nity has simr ly carried on and taken over these pagan symhols which antedated Christianity.—Which part of the Bible does a Spiritualist reject and which part does he accept P—ln regard to matters of faith or belief, in Spiritualism every ‘human being is free to think for him- self. Personally we may believe in one thing or another. There are Spiritualists who are materialists, agnostics, and some have come from the Salvation Army and others fe use the Church of England and the Nonconformist bodies. We leave them free to believe as troth compels them to believe.—On what authority does a Spiritullist rejoct the existence of Jesus Christ e— With regard to the existence of Jesus Christ I say I don't know. It ie a question of evidence. Outside the Bible we have really no historical evidence that such an individual ever lived.—What is the neces- sity for dark seances ?—God is the author of darkness as well as light. Wo have a dark seance every night. There are certain facts which can be observed in the dark. The photographer requires a darkl room to de- velop his plat s, and fireworks can be seen to the best advantage at night time. There are manifestations which can be best observed under these conditions. It is hardly fair to misjudge Spiritualists on account of dark seances. For many years Spiritualists have been advised not to sit in the dark, and this was being dis— continued.—What advice can be g' ven to anyone wish- ing to investigate P—Anyone who wants advice can get the rules of the opirit circle by applying to the members of the Warrington society.-11 departed spirits commis, 'skate through mediums how is it that many who would be gladly convinced have fail, to get satisfactory proof ?—I have never met an individual who has failed to obtain proof. It nine be a long time coming or it may come quickly, but if you wilt investigate in the right spirit you are bound to get it sooner or later. —Can a spirit be materialised 2—Nia. People call manifestations materialised spirits. A spirit cannot be meterialised but it can build up a temporary physicalisecl form. Did it ever strike you what we are ourselves Are we not materialised forms due to spirit A spirit forms our body. In its development invisible subs'ances and forces are employerl.—Dnes hypnotism hear any part in mediumship P— The only difference is this, that in hypnotism the person is in the body, and in the case of Spiritualism the indivi- dual is out of the body—Shall you accept the challenge to meet Mr. Waldron P—When Mr. Waldron has I. waited, cgarnined, and investigated "and speaks from knowledge I may be prepared to meet him as my equal on the platform. (Laughter)—Can any Spiritualist when in trance condition speak in a foreign language or be under the guidance fsa foreign spirit ?—I don't see why there should he anycliffic.ulty about this. Those who read their Bible will know that on the day of Pente- cost, the apletles spoke in the languages of the spirits controlling them. They would find that the Bible was full of similar cases. The Irving church was founded upon it. Some mediums were controlled to speak in foreign languages, being controlled he foreign spirits. He did not say that all mediums spoke that way, but some did, and many had been identiSscl. They were duplicating the testimony of the Bible.—Can you explain why an uneducated medium in repeating the supposed communications from a spirit should los ungrammatical, if such is verbatimP—lt an educated man gives a mess- age to an uneducated message boy that boy will give it in his own way. The medium is the errand boy of the spirit. . . _</p>
        <p>-This was the last of the questions asked,and the meet- ing dispersed. THE REV. C. J. MILLAR AND TFIE CONTROVERSY; Preaching at the Emmanuel Church on Sunday morn- ing, the Vioar-in-charge, ttio Rev. C.J. Millar, alluded to the recent niintroversy 6.ri Christianity and Spiritualisna in Parr Hall. Be said to ask vrhether Spiritualism was a •better erred than Christianity was to ask whether the light of a tallow candle ta britahter and clearer than the Light of the maimday sun. Spiritualism had added noth- ing to thr %acrid's lato:.•k of knowledge. It had no cer- tainty •, It had twitter a Ciehsemane nor a eAlvary. It was a feeble little candle, and they were asked to ex- change the noonday splendour of the Sun of Righteous- i nem for its flickering and uncertain ray.</p>
