      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN PHILHARMONIC SOCIETYS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PHILHARMONIC SOCIETYS</p>
        <p>CONCERT.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday eveoing in t—he Ruocero Public Hall,tho Runcorn Pinkie rnionic Society (the amalgamated choirs at the Musical Society and tho Tonic Sol-fa Prize Choir), gave their necond concert. The attendance was not &amp;- together encouraging, as the back of tha hall on the ground floor was nearly empty, and the seen upstairs were only filled once round. The performance was of a miscellaneous character, and from a musical point of view it was thoroughly successful, and one in which a. high etandard of excellence was reached. The principals were Mr. J. P. Robinson, Mr. L. N. Burch, Mr. Salkeld. and Mr. Charles Fry. Mr. L. N. Burch in hie gene " The • Two Grenadiers" (Schumann), commencing "fo Franse there journey'el two Grenaaliers," displayed that he had had a careful teaching, and is the possessor of a well cultured baritone voice. He was fairly received. The horn soloist, Mr. B. Salkeld gave "Rondo " (from E flat Co ncerte, Mczart). Me showed that he had obtained a complete mastery over the instrument. The audience vociferously applauded Mr. Charles Fry when he ap- peared on the stage end he recited "The Belle" (E. A. Poe) with piano accompaniment in no. style. He is. most exceedingly clever bath in style eel r.tanner. leavini, the staee he was loudly applauded and had to return] when he eetertainecl the company with another recitation. For his rendition of tee song "The Sailors Grave" (Sullivan) commencing with the words "There- is,in the wide lone sea," Mr. Robinson received a warm greeting. In reply to the encore he gave "Kind and gentle my young Mary." At this part of the programme Mr. Wesley C. C.oke,onei of the secretaries, appeared on the platform, and an- nounced that Mr. Fry had kindly consented to give. another recitation before the first part of the gramme concluded. This announcement was received witii much satisfaction and Mr. Fry's appearance on the etege was again loudly applauded. He gave the recitatioi, entitled "The Revenge." In the second part Mr.Bilree svas warmly applauded for his rendition of " A soldier's grave " (Mascheroni). He was obliged to re-appear and give another. " Ave Maria" (Schubert); a horn-solo- by Mr. Salkeld with piano accompaniment was well ap- plauded, and in reply Mr. Salkeld bowed his acknow- ledgments. Messrs. Burch and Robinson were encored for the duett " Ladies Fly from Lows'. Smo.th Tale." They replied with " Excelsior " " The Charity Diener '" (Mosley) by fdr.Fry caused roars of laughter.ln reeponse to an encore he gave" The Hiudoo's Paradise " an I oa his retiring he audience had scarcely recovered themselves when the next item was proceeded with. " Toe Last Watch " (Pinsuti) givea by Mr. Robinson, commencing with, " Watch with me, bye, to-night," was the last .of the nieces given by the leading artistes. The introductory chorus was very effectively (nine, and the same may be said of The Blue Bettie', Fate," (Ashworth). Th.; part songs " To Sylvia " (Schubert), " Jack Frost," (Hatton), and " March of the men of Harleeh " (Old Welsh) were very charmingly rendered as testified to by the plaudit' they received. The concert, musically, was a decided success. The Chorus numbered 100 voices, and Mr. Granville Ban-- took used the baton, Mr. B. Musgrove presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
