        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET, BOWLING, AND ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL C.LFTB, PROPOSED PURCHASE OF THE CRICKET GROUND. At:the Patten Arms Hotel, Worrington, last week, an extraordinary general meeting of the members of the above-mentioned club was held for the pur- pose of receiving the report of the committee ap- pointed at the last extraordinary general meeting with reference to the proposed purchase of the Cricket Ground. There was a good attendance,and the proceedings were of a harmonious and even enthusiastic character. Mr. R. T. PAIRCLOUGH was appointed to take the chair, and called upon the hon. sec,(Mr.C.H.Unsworth) to read the report of the committee, which ran as fol- lows :--" Report of the committee appointed to go into the question of purchase of the Cricket Ground, R. T. Faircl, ugh, William Richardson, John Mainwaring, C. Leigh, R. Silcosk, and C. H. Unsworth (hon. sec.) Gentlemen.—we beg to report tbat most of the honorary and playing members have been canvassed and promises obtained to take shares in the Proposed company to the extent of £2,189. The total cost of the ground including the expenses of formation of the proposed company etc., is estimated at £2,900. Having regard to the number of promises received the committee recommend that the purchase be proceeded With and that a few gentlemen, net exceeding seven, be appointed by this meeting to carry out the necessary arrangements for completing the purchase, and that these gentlemen act as first directors of the company. The committee also suggest that any balance required for completing the purchase be raised by loan on the security of the property of the company,and the amount ao raised be lent to the Cricket club at the same rate of interest at which it is borrowed, repayable in such stunner as may be thereafter agreed upon between the company and the club." On the proposition of Mr. T. Bramus,seconded by Mr. W. H. j. JENKINS, the report was unanimously adopted. The following gentlemen were appointed to carry out the necessary arrangements for completing the purchase, and to act asthe first directors of the proposed company 'CZ Messrs. R. T Fairclough, W. Richardson, W. H Robinson, C. Leigh,J. L. Tunstall, R. Silcock, and J. tlainwaring. The CHAIRMAN in the course of a short speech stated that it was intended, if possible, to raise the whole of tke purchase money in shares and that as this object had been so nearly accomplished he trusted that all the Members present would use influence with their friends to get them to take up the few remaining shares, parti- cularly pointing out that it was not necessary for every Shareholder to be a member of the club, but the shares would be allotted to any applicant who had any regard for the welfare and prosperity of the club on application to the hon. secretary. (Applause ) Seversl members present intimated their intention to take up shares, and additional shares to the extent of £BO were subscribed in the room, making che total amount of share capital promised, £2,271. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Purchasing Committee for their services, and the Chairman's ser- vices having also been acknowledged, the meeting ter- minated.</p>
